<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the most well-known theoretical results in the study of neural networks is the universal approximation theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cybenko, 1989</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carroll and Dickinson, 1989</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallant and White, 1992</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Irie and Miyake, 1988</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Funahashi, 1989</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hornik et al., 1990</a>)</cite>, which states that neural networks can approximate any continuous function, to any desired accuracy, uniformly on any compact subset of <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This result forms the cornerstone of the approximation theory of neural networks. However, the universal approximation theorem doesn’t provide any quantitative information concerning the size of the required neural network, limiting its practical utility. To address this issue, later research focused on deriving approximation <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rates</em> for different types of neural networks over a variety of well-behaved function classes. For example, quantitative approximation rates have been derived for both shallow neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barron, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Makovoz, 1996</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bach, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klusowski and Barron, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mhaskar, 1996</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrushev, 1998</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel and Xu, 2024</a>)</cite>, and deep ReLU neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gühring et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petersen and Voigtlaender, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel, 2023</a>)</cite>. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeVore et al. (2021)</a></cite> for an overview of the approximation theory of neural networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One of the main problems of interest in this direction is to determine optimal approximation rates for deep ReLU neural networks on classical smoothness spaces, such as Besov and Sobolev spaces. In the series of papers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite>, this problem has been given a complete solution — at least for deep ReLU neural networks — in terms of the total number of parameters. A special case of these results is the following. Suppose that a function <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,1]^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,1]^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder. Then <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated to accuracy <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly on <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> by a deep ReLU neural network with <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cdot(1/\epsilon)^{N/2\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cdot(1/\epsilon)^{N/2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters, where <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C(N,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C(N,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, this rate cannot be improved asymptotically as <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This quantifies exactly how large a deep ReLU network must be in order to achieve a certain approximation error, depending upon the smoothness of the target function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The purpose of this paper is to address the problem of quantitative approximation rates for <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">group equivariant</span> and <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">invariant</span> neural networks. This is a popular paradigm in the recent machine learning literature, where function spaces for machine learning tasks are designed to have the same group symmetries as the target function which is being approximated. For example, in image classification tasks, the goal is to map an image <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to a vector <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> representing the class the image belongs to (e.g., dog, cat,…). This task is invariant to translations, that is, <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(gx)=f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(gx)=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every translation <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. In other learning settings, the function <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to be learned is <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> equivariant, meaning that applying a group transformation to the input is equivalent to applying the same transformation to the output: <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(gx)=g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(gx)=g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Group equivariant learning refers to the practice of designing function classes, i.e., neural network architectures, which by design have the same invariant/equivariant structure as the functions they are designed to approximate. Examples include convolutional neural networks, which respect the translation symmetry of images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang, 2019</a>)</cite>, graph neural networks which respect the permutation symmetries of graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maron et al., 2019a</a>)</cite>, neural networks for unordered point sets which respect their permutation symmetries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, and their extension to point clouds and geometric graphs which additionally require symmetries to rigid motions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Satorras et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gasteiger et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. More exotic examples include multi-permutation equivariance for problems where the input itself is a neural network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Navon et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Lorenz-equivariance for physical simulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bogatskiy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> equivariance for polynomial optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lawrence and Harris, 2024</a>)</cite> and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SU}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SU}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> equivariance for quantum neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. By hard-coding symmetries into the network architecture, one can improve sample efficiency and out-of-distribution generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elesedy and Zaidi, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Batzner et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The development of equivariant models has been significantly influenced by theoretical research into their approximation capabilities. Research so far has focused on universality, that is, proving that a given equivariant model is universal, i.e., can approximate all equivariant functions. This guarantees that there exists a configuration of model parameters that is a good approximation of the target function (even if finding these parameters can be difficult due to limited optimization and generalization capabilities). For example, the pioneering works on multisets, DeepSets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and PointNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, both proved their models’ universality for multiset approximation. For graphs, the approximation abilities of <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">message passing neural networks (MPNNs)</em> have inspired standard networks like GIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, while their limitations inspired many popular graph neural networks with improved approximation power <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maron et al., 2019a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keriven and Peyré, 2019</a>)</cite>. Universality of networks which are jointly equivariant to permutations and rigid motions was discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym and Maron (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hordan et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delle Rose et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hordan et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">While universal approximation of equivariant networks is well-studied, very little is known regarding more quantitative estimates of their approximation rates. This is an interesting direction, because while both equivariant and non-equivariant models have been proven to be universal over broad model classes, their quantitative approximation rates have not been rigorously compared. In a sense, we ask: do equivariant models impart benefits via improved <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">efficiency</em> of approximation, i.e. greater expressivity for a given number of parameters? Or, is it solely a matter of sample efficiency and optimization? In this work, we take the first steps towards addressing this gap.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now give a somewhat informal description of our results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our focus is on analyzing approximation rates of functions <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on a Euclidean vector space <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, which are invariant, or equivariant, to the action of a group <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition to the assumption that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is equivariant, we will assume that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder for some <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the Hölder assumption, it is known that ReLU networks with <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N/2\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N/2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> neurons can approximate <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy. The first question we ask is whether the additional assumption of invariance/equivariance can reduce this approximation rate for ReLU networks (which are not equivariant by design). The intuition is that, since the value of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on a point <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> determines its value on all of its orbit, the exponential dependence on the dimension of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> should replaced with an exponential dependence on the dimension of the orbit space <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math>, which we denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> (the notion of dimension used here will be discussed in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Namely, we expect approximation rates of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N_{G}/2\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N_{G}/2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second question we consider is whether contemporary architectures, which are invariant or equivariant by design, can attain approximation rates of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N_{G}/2\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N_{G}/2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. A priori, a positive answer to the second question and a negative answer to the first question would show that the incorporation of equivariance into the model leads to more efficient approximation. Alternatively, a negative answer to the second question and a positive answer to the first question would imply that enforcing equivariance as a hard constraint leads to inferior approximation, which would be in line with other recent arguments in the literature that favor data augmentation or approximate equivariance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tahmasebi and Weber, </a>)</cite>. However, we ultimately find that in the scenarios we consider in this paper, the answers to both questions are positive. In other words, generic ReLU networks and specialized invariant networks achieve roughly the same expressivity (over invariant functions) as a function of network size. Therefore, differences in their practical behavior are not attributable to expressivity <em id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">over invariant functions</em>, but instead to differences in optimization and sample complexity deriving from the distinct, restricted parameterization of invariant networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First setting: permutation invariant and equivariant models</span> Our analysis can be divided into three different settings. In the first setting, we consider <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">point sets</span>. Point sets (also referred to as point clouds), are <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices which we think of as a collection of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points in <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">A permutation <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by reordering of the points, namely</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right)=\left(x_{\tau(1)},\ldots,x_{\tau(n)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right)=\left(x_{\tau(1)},\ldots,x_{\tau(n)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Since the permutation group is finite, the dimensions of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are the same. Accordingly, our first question is irrelevant in this setting: ReLU networks will have the approximation rates we desire, as <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}=N</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, only the second question is relevant here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We focus first on the well known DeepSets permutation invariant model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, which enforces permutation invariance by applying the same ReLU neural network <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> independently to each point <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then summing all points and applying an additional “outer” ReLU network <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in the total formula</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{i})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Despite the simplicity of this model, and the fact that it cannot directly process interactions between points, it was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al. (2017)</a></cite> that DeepSets are universal, in the sense that they can approximate all permutation invariant functions. However, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zweig and Bruna (2022)</a></cite> argued that these models have inferior approximation abilities with respect to models which process pairwise interactions, like Set Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Accordingly, it is not <em id="S1.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a priori</em> clear whether DeepSets can attain the same approximation rates as ReLU networks. Our first main result is that this is indeed the case: DeepSets can approximate permutation invariant functions which are <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with the expected approximation rate of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N_{G}/2\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim(1/\epsilon)^{N_{G}/2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, where in this setting <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}=N=nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}=N=nd</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, these results also hold when the domain <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is not a vector space but a subspace of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose “intrinsic” dimension is <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As a second step, we also show how to leverage our results for DeepSets to prove similar approximation rates for two popular permutation <span id="S1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">equivariant</span> models, Set Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and Sumformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Segol and Lipman, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As a corollary to our result on DeepSets, we additionally obtain a result of independent interest, relating to the approximation of non-invariant functions by ReLU networks. This relates to the question of approximation rates over a compact domain <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> whose “intrinsic” dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower than the ambient dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. In several works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaham et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labate and Shi, 2024</a>)</cite>, it was shown that if <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional manifold residing in <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, then a <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder function <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:K\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:K\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by a ReLU network with <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters, namely the network size depends on the intrinsic dimension rather than the ambient dimension. A more general result which does not assume <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a manifold, but only that it has Minkowski dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math>, was proven by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakada and Imaizumi (2020)</a></cite>. In contrast, we will show in this paper that we can get an approximation rate of <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an improvement by a factor of two (inside the exponent) over the <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> factor obtained in previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakada and Imaizumi, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second setting: Models jointly invariant to permutations and rigid motions</span>
In this setting, we consider again point clouds <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where now we are interested in invariance not only to permutations, but also to the group of rigid motions <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, for a given orthogonal matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and vector <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider functions which are invariant to the action</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,t)\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right)=\left(Rx_{1}+t,\ldots,Rx_{n}+t\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,t)\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right)=\left(Rx_{1}+t,\ldots,Rx_{n}+t\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">This type of symmetry typically occurs in dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, where the point set could be a representation of a 3D object in computer vision or a set of <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> particles in particle dynamics. There are many models in the recent machine learning literature taking these symmetries into account; prominent examples include EGNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Satorras et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, Dimenet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gasteiger et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Vector Neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and MACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Batatia et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, the dimension of the quotient space <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}=V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}=V/G</annotation></semantics></math> will be lower than the dimension of <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, namely</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}=nd-\mathrm{dim}(E(d))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}=nd-\mathrm{dim}(E(d)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, we begin by addressing our first question, and show that ReLU networks can attain the desired approximation rates in this invariant setting. We then leverage these results, as well as our results from DeepSets, to show that invariant neural networks, based on applying rotation invariant frames to DeepSets, attain the desired approximation rates. Examples of such networks include networks based on alignment to the shapes’ principal directions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al., </a>)</cite> and networks based on weighted frame averaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third Setting: Bi-Lipschitz invariant models</span>
In this setting, we consider a more general setting of closed isometry groups acting on <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the invariant models we consider obey the paradigm of bi-Lipschitz invariant models. We show that such models always attain the desired approximation rates. Examples of bi-Lipschitz models include max filter based models for finite groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mixon and Packer, 2023</a>)</cite>, permutation invariant models based on multidimension sorting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balan et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Davidson and Dym, 2024</a>)</cite> or the FSW embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amir and Dym, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sverdlov et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and models for anti-symmetric functions based on bi-Lipschitz invariants for the alternating group <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Our main results are summarized in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1.1 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span> <span id="S1.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of main results. Rates denote the asymptotic number of parameters
needed for <math id="S1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-approximation as <math id="S1.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> (up to logarithmic factors). Here, <math id="S1.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of input elements of dimension <math id="S1.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">intrinsic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the intrinsic dimension of the “data domain” quotiented by the group (see equation (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)).</span></figcaption>
<table id="S1.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S1.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"></th>
<th id="S1.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Permutations</span><span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (</span><math id="S1.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><a href="#S3" title="3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S1.T1.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Perm. &amp; rigid motions</span><span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (</span><math id="S1.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">,
</span><a href="#S4" title="4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.1.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S1.T1.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">General isometries </span><span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (</span><math id="S1.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\leq O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">G</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\leq O(N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><a href="#S5" title="5 Bi-Lipschitz invariant models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.1.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S1.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:62.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Q1: ReLU</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.T1.4.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> finite, so standard rate of </span><math id="S1.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (ReLU approximation rates for 𝑂(𝑑) invariant functions). ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.2.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.2.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: </span><math id="S1.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: </span><math id="S1.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:62.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.4.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Q2: Equivariant</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.4.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Theorems </span><a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Definition of the Transformer Architecture ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: DeepSets, Sumformer, and Transformer, </span><math id="S1.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Approximation rates for rotation invariant function spaces using frames ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: frame + ReLU, </span><math id="S1.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 5 Bi-Lipschitz invariant models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a><span id="S1.T1.4.3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: bi-Lipschitz invariants, </span><math id="S1.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion and Future Work</span>
We believe our results in these three different settings are an important step towards a more quantitative approximation theory for group equivariant learning, as they establish for the first time approximation rates for several prominent models defined on equivariant data. Moreover, the ability of non-invariant ReLU networks to exploit group invariance and achieve expected approximation rates is consistent with empirical observations that, given sufficient data, non-invariant models can compete effectively with those that incorporate rotation invariance by design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quiroga et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brehmer et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This suggests that the benefits of hard-coded symmetries may manifest more significantly in sample efficiency and optimization rather than in asymptotic expressivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">However, there remain several open directions for future work. Firstly, while we achieve approximation rates for rotation equivariant frames, we do not have approximation rates for many popular rotation equivariant models such as EGNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Satorras et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, Dimenet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gasteiger et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Vector Neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and MACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Batatia et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. It would be interesting to establish approximation rates for these models, and see whether this analysis can yield provable gaps in the approximation rates between different equivariant methods. In such discussion one needs to take into account that many of these models may not even be universal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, unless some additional restrictions to the domain are applied <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hordan et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sverdlov and Dym, 2025</a>)</cite>. Simultaneously, it is possible that the study of asymptotic approximation rates, which ignores constants, is too coarse a measure to capture meaningful difference between models at realistic scales, and a still more refined analysis is necessary in order to, e.g, substantiate gaps in approximation quality between different equivariant models, or between equivariant and non-equivariant models. Examples of this type of analysis for the permutation group include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zweig and Bruna (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zweig and Bruna (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approximation rates for permutation invariant models</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in the introduction, seminal works such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maron et al. (2019b)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al. (2017)</a></cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2022)</a></cite> establish the universality of various invariant architectures, but do not provide quantitative approximation rates. Before this work, the only quantitative approximation rates we were aware of for invariant functions pertained to the permutation group. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2022)</a></cite> provides explicit rates for permutation invariant functions with feature dimension scaling as <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{Nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{Nd}</annotation></semantics></math>, but the constructions are not necessarily implementable as neural architectures, requiring an arbitrary per-element featurization. We provide rates for architectures with the same asymptotics in <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> that are implementable as practical neural networks for <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder functions. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bachmayr et al. (2024)</a></cite> use invariant polynomials to approximate <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">analytic</em> invariant functions, with an error exponential in the polynomial degree. Such results can be combined with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite> in order to obtain neural network rates. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wagstaff et al. (2022)</a></cite> characterize necessary and sufficient embedding dimensions for universal set function approximation, providing lower bounds that illuminate the role of the latent dimension in DeepSets-type architectures. On the negative side, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zweig and Bruna (2022)</a></cite> demonstrate exponential separations between symmetric neural networks that process only element-wise features versus those with access to pairwise interactions, suggesting fundamental limitations of DeepSets relative to higher-order models. Our results show that despite this separation, DeepSets still achieves the optimal <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymptotic</em> approximation rate over Hölder functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is also a parallel line of work on the approximation of permutation-equivariant functions by the transformer architecture. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al. (2020)</a></cite> established that transformers are universal over permutation-equivariant functions, for fixed width but arbitrary depth. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Takeshita and Imaizumi (2025)</a></cite> derives approximation rates for transformers on symmetric polynomials, with more favorable dependence on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, though restricted to polynomial targets rather than the general Hölder function class considered here.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intrinsic dimension and neural network approximation</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A related line of work studies how the approximation complexity of ReLU networks can be reduced when the domain has low intrinsic dimension. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaham et al. (2018)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2019)</a></cite> showed that if the domain is a smooth manifold of dimension <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> embedded in <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the approximation rate depends on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labate and Shi (2024)</a></cite> extended these results with refined generalization bounds. In a more general setting not requiring manifold structure, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakada and Imaizumi (2020)</a></cite> proved that approximation rates depend on the Minkowski dimension of the domain, obtaining a rate of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder functions. We improve on this result by a factor of two in the exponent, obtaining <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/(2\alpha)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/(2\alpha)}</annotation></semantics></math>. These intrinsic dimension results are directly relevant to equivariant approximation: for a group <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acting freely on a space <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, the orbit space <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(V)-\dim(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(V)-\dim(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, and our framework exploits this reduction, connecting equivariant approximation to approximation over lower-dimensional quotient spaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bi-Lipschitz invariant models and frame averaging</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results on bi-Lipschitz invariant models (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Bi-Lipschitz invariant models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) build upon a growing body of work constructing provably bi-Lipschitz invariant embeddings. For finite subgroups of the orthogonal group, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al. (2024b)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mixon and Packer (2023)</a></cite> develop the theory of group-invariant max filtering, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mixon and Qaddura (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qaddura (2026)</a></cite> establish injectivity and local bi-Lipschitz properties of these maps on orbit spaces. General theoretical frameworks have been proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al. (2024a)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balan and Tsoukanis (2023)</a></cite>. For the permutation group specifically, concrete bi-Lipschitz embeddings include those based on sorting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balan et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Davidson and Dym, 2024</a>)</cite>, the Fourier Sliced-Wasserstein embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amir and Dym, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sverdlov et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and bi-Lipschitz invariants for the alternating group <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In a complementary direction, frame averaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al., </a>)</cite> provides a general recipe for achieving exact invariance by averaging over a discrete set of group elements, with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2024)</a></cite> showing that continuous canonicalization is impossible for commonly used groups, motivating weighted frames <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti, 2023</a>)</cite>. Our work provides the first quantitative approximation rates for models based on these bi-Lipschitz and frame averaging approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization perspective</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While this paper focuses on approximation rates, a complementary line of work establishes that equivariance yields provable generalization benefits. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elesedy and Zaidi (2021)</a></cite> proved strict generalization improvements for equivariant models, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei et al. (2021)</a></cite> showed that invariance can save a factor of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in sample complexity, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sannai et al. (2021)</a></cite> derived tighter bounds via quotient feature spaces. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrache and Trivedi (2023)</a></cite> analyzed approximation-generalization trade-offs under approximate group equivariance. Our finding that equivariant and non-equivariant models achieve the same asymptotic approximation rates over invariant functions complements these results, suggesting that the practical advantages of equivariant architectures are primarily driven by sample efficiency rather than differences in expressivity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Paper Structure</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of the remainder of the paper is as follows: In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we discuss some mathematical preliminaries necessary for a full statement and proof of our results. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we discuss our results for the first setting, models invariant or equivariant to the action of permutations. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we discuss our results for the second setting: invariant to both permutations and rigid motions, and in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Bi-Lipschitz invariant models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we discuss the third setting: bi-Lipschitz models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we give several formal definitions necessary for a full formal statement and proof of our main results later on.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation</span>
We will use <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> without any subscript to denote the standard two-norm on vectors. <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the Frobenius matrix norm.
We will use <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the group of permutations on <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements. <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the group of orthogonal <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrices and <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the group generated by orthogonal transformations and translations by vectors in <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The group of special orthogonal matrices (orthogonal matrices with determinant of 1) is denoted by <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(d)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Invariance and Equivariance</span>
Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be two sets, and <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a group acting on these sets. We say that <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">equivariant</em> if <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(gv)=gf(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(gv)=gf(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant in the case where the action of <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, namely <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g.w=w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g.w=w</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G,w\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G,w\in W</annotation></semantics></math>, so that we obtain <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(gv)=f(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(gv)=f(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regularity</span>
We suppose that in addition to being invariant or equivariant, the function <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies a certain <em id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothness condition</em>. In this work, we will measure smoothness in the Hölder spaces. Given an <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder semi-norm of a function <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{C^{\alpha}}:=\sup_{x\neq y}\frac{|f(x)-f(y)|}{\|x-y\|^{\alpha}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{C^{\alpha}}:=\sup_{x\neq y}\frac{|f(x)-f(y)|}{\|x-y\|^{\alpha}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We will suppose that the target function <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{C^{\alpha}}\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{C^{\alpha}}\leq C</annotation></semantics></math> for some fixed constant <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and is invariant or equivariant. We can more generally consider smoothness through the modulus of continuity, defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(f,\epsilon):=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}x,y\in\Omega\\
|x-y|\leq\epsilon\end{subarray}}|f(x)-f(y)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(f,\epsilon):=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}x,y\in\Omega\\
|x-y|\leq\epsilon\end{subarray}}|f(x)-f(y)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Hölder continuity is then just equivalent to <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(f,\epsilon)\leq C\epsilon^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(f,\epsilon)\leq C\epsilon^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. We remark that these notions depend upon a choice of norm <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, which we by default typically taken to the Euclidean norm. However, in some of our results it will be more convenient to use the <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm on <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> instead. In any case, the choice of norm will only affect the final results by changing the constants involved, due to the equivalence of norms in a finite dimensional space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let us remark that using the modulus of continuity only allows our analysis to consider smoothness of order up to a single derivative. In order to handle higher order smoothness, we would also need to consider higher order moduli of smoothness (see for instance Chapter 2 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeVore and Lorentz (1993)</a></cite>). However, this will introduce significant technical complications in our arguments, and for the sake of simplicity we leave this for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quotient space</span> For a group <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acting on a set <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the orbit of an element <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[v]</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of orbits is denoted by <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> and we refer to it as the quotient space. The quotient map <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:V\to V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:V\to V/G</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapping</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(v)=[v]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(v)=[v].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subgroup of the group of isometries of <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, namely <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can define a metric on the quotient space <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> via</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}([x],[y])=\min_{g\in G}\|gx-y\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}([x],[y])=\min_{g\in G}\|gx-y\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Since the elements of <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are isometries, this definition does not depend on the choice of representatives <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X,y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X,y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> and is a well defined metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Covering Number and Dimension</span>
Our results will search for an approximation of a Holder function on a compact set <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> using neural networks. The size of the networks will be strongly tied to the covering number of <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall that for a metric space <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,d)</annotation></semantics></math>, and a number <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X,r)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the smallest number of balls of radius <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which form a cover of <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, when <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=[0,1]^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=[0,1]^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the covering number of <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies, for an appropriate positive constant <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequality</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K)\leq Cr^{-N},\quad\forall r&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K)\leq Cr^{-N},\quad\forall r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">As we are interested in <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> invariant and equivariant functions, we will be able to think of our functions as functions defined on the quotient space <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will be interested in the covering number of <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the quotient metric <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Generally speaking, we will expect to get an equation of the form (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), where <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is replace with <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-\dim(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-\dim(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. The intuition for this is as follows: assume that <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lie Group, with a free and proper action on <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that a proper action means that the map <math id="S2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(g,x)=(gx,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(g,x)=(gx,x)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}\times\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}\times\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, which in turn means that for every compact set <math id="S2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{N}\times\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{N}\times\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the preimage <math id="S2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{-1}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{-1}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> will be compact. A free action means that if <math id="S2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gx=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gx=x</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity element in <math id="S2.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, the quotient space is a manifold (see e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duistermaat and Kolk (2012)</a></cite>), with a dimension of</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathbb{R}^{N}/G)=N-\dim(G)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathbb{R}^{N}/G)=N-\dim(G),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, if we wish to approximate <math id="S2.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math>, a compact subset of <math id="S2.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}/G</annotation></semantics></math>, then we will indeed be able to get a bound as in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the point cloud setting we are interested in, the groups are Lie groups, and their action is proper, but it is typically not free. Thus, the dimension equality in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) may not hold. For example, in the case <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}/O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}/O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> (since the orbit of a vector <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sphere of radius <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math>), and so has dimension</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathbb{R}^{d}/O(d))=1&gt;d-\frac{d(d-1)}{2}=\dim(\mathbb{R}^{d})-\dim(O(d)),\quad\forall d\geq 3" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathbb{R}^{d}/O(d))=1&gt;d-\frac{d(d-1)}{2}=\dim(\mathbb{R}^{d})-\dim(O(d)),\quad\forall d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Nonetheless, in the cases we focus on, the covering number of (compact subsets of) <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}/G</annotation></semantics></math> does scale like <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dn-\dim(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dn-\dim(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (despite the fact that <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}/G</annotation></semantics></math> is not a manifold)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para"><span id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{prop}</span>
<p id="S2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">[Proof in appendix]
Let <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,n</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers and let <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group acting on <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume that one of the following holds:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the permutation group, <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=E(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=E(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.p11.3" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> invariant set <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math> is compact,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K/G)\leq Cr^{-(nd-\dim(G))},\quad\forall r&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K/G)\leq Cr^{-(nd-\dim(G))},\quad\forall r&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.4" class="ltx_p">In the first case where <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the group is finite and so has dimension zero. In the second case <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> does have positive dimension.
This proposition gives the intuition as to why the approximation rates we derive for permutation groups in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and for <math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> are the “correct” rates. We stress however that these results do not depend on this proposition. The assumption that <math id="S2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>
is not very restrictive as in applications typically <math id="S2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3,n\gg 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3,n\gg 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We also note that an assumption like (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is almost equivalent to asserting that the upper-Minkowsi dimension of <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the upper-Minkowski dimension of a set <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathrm{dim}}\left(K\right)=\limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\frac{\log(N(K,r))}{\log(1/r)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>dim</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathrm{dim}}\left(K\right)=\limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\frac{\log(N(K,r))}{\log(1/r)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p12.2" class="ltx_p">If (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) holds, it follows that <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathrm{dim}}\left(K\right)\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>dim</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathrm{dim}}\left(K\right)\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, if
<math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathrm{dim}}\left(K\right)=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>dim</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathrm{dim}}\left(K\right)=N</annotation></semantics></math>, this implies a slightly weaker statement: namely, that for all <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a constant <math id="S2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> such that,</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(r)\leq C(1/r)^{d+\eta}\quad\forall r\in(0,1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(r)\leq C(1/r)^{d+\eta}\quad\forall r\in(0,1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider approximation rates for permutation-invariant and equivariant functions. Technically, the most significant result in this section is the approximation rate we derive for the permutation-invariant DeepSets model. These results are later used to derive approximation rates for two popular permutation equivariant architectures: Sumformer and Set Transformer. We will then obtain as a corollary improved approximation rates for non-invariant <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder functions defined on domains with ‘low’ Minkowski dimension.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by deriving quantitative approximation rates for the Deep Sets architecture introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al. (2017)</a></cite>. In fact, it will be convenient to consider the following generalization of Deep Sets, which will prove useful when analyzing permutation equivariant architectures. Specifically, consider the index set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]:=\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]:=\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\{P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\{P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a partition of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the group</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}:=\{\sigma\in S_{n}:~\sigma(P_{i})=P_{i}~\text{for $i=1,...,k$}\}\subset S_{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}:=\{\sigma\in S_{n}:~\sigma(P_{i})=P_{i}~\text{for $i=1,...,k$}\}\subset S_{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is isomorphic to a product of symmetric groups on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> elements, i.e.</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}\eqsim\prod_{i=1}^{k}S_{|P_{i}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≂</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}\eqsim\prod_{i=1}^{k}S_{|P_{i}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For a given partition <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider functions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which are invariant to permutations in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. The case where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\{[n]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\{[n]\}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to full permutation invariance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant neural networks which are of the form</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi(x_{i})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi(x_{i})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are parameterized by ReLU neural networks. When <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\{[n]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\{[n]\}</annotation></semantics></math>, this reduces to the DeepSets architecture introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al. (2017)</a></cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{i})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We remark that in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) we could have also use a different network <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> for each block of the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, but we will actually show that this is not necessary to obtain our approximation rates (and universality). We also remark that the groups <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> are examples of groups for which first order networks are universal (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maron et al. (2019b)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We wish to obtain approximation rates using the architecture (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) where the networks <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> are parameterized by deep ReLU neural networks. In order to obtain quantitative rates, we must make quantitative assumptions on the target function <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. As remarked in the introduction (see (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)), we will measure the complexity of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> using the modulus of continuity with respect to the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm on the domain <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(f,\epsilon):=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}x,y\in\Omega\\
\|x-y\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\end{subarray}}|f(x)-f(y)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(f,\epsilon):=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}x,y\in\Omega\\
\|x-y\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\end{subarray}}|f(x)-f(y)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Our main result is the following upper bound on the achievable approximation rates.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a partition of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant function. Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant subset, and denote by <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> the smallest number of balls (in the <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm) of radius <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> required to cover <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> modulo the action of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer and set <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2dn+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2dn+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exist ReLU neural networks <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying that for every <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|f(X)-\rho\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi(x_{i})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|f(X)-\rho\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi(x_{i})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The network </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has three hidden layers of width </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2d(2dn+1)(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2d(2dn+1)(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, followed by one hidden layer of width </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and an output layer of width </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2nd+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2nd+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The network </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> consists of </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> layers of width </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> followed by </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> layers of width </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=12(2dn+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=12(2dn+1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{1}{2m(2nd+1)},\quad L\leq C\max\{\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log[n(2m+1)N_{r}(K)]},\log(2nd+1)^{2}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{1}{2m(2nd+1)},\quad L\leq C\max\{\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log[n(2m+1)N_{r}(K)]},\log(2nd+1)^{2}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an absolute constant.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Before giving the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, let us note the following corollary. Here we consider the case of Deep Sets approximating permutation invariant functions, i.e., the partition into a single set containing all elements, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\{[n]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\{[n]\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The assumptions on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuity, i.e., that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(f,t)\leq t^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(f,t)\leq t^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rates are given in terms of the intrinsic dimension of the set <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,d,d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,d,d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> be fixed natural numbers with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2d+2</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be real numbers. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a permutation invariant set with</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K/S_{n})\leq C_{1}r^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}},\quad\forall r&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K/S_{n})\leq C_{1}r^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}},\quad\forall r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2dn+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2dn+1</annotation></semantics></math> there exist ReLU neural networks <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with a total number of parameters</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\leq C\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/(2\alpha)}(1+|\log\epsilon|)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq C\epsilon^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}/(2\alpha)}(1+|\log\epsilon|),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">such that for any permutation invariant function <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(f,t)\leq t^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(f,t)\leq t^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> there is a choice of parameters such that</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|f(X)-\rho\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{i})\right)\right|\leq\epsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|f(X)-\rho\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{i})\right)\right|\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant depending only on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,n,d_{\text{intrinsic}},C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,n,d_{\text{intrinsic}},C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, but not on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.

This result follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by setting <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m25" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m=C\epsilon^{-1/\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=C\epsilon^{-1/\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, counting the number of parameters in the networks <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, and noting that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d_{\text{intrinsic}}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d_{\text{intrinsic}}/2}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates both <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2d+2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, in the case that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have that (<a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) holds with <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}=nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}=nd</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the mild assumption that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> we have that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nd\geq 2d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nd\geq 2d+2</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and so
Corollary <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> implies that on the class of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous functions in dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=dn</annotation></semantics></math>, Deep Sets matches up to a logarithmic factor the optimal approximation rates for general ReLU MLPs (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2022)</a></cite>). This indicates that there is no loss of expressivity when using the Deep Sets architecture to enforce permutation invariance.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
First, let us note that we may assume without loss of generality that the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> consists of consecutive pieces, i.e., if <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}:=|P_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}:=|P_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}=\{n_{1}+n_{2}+...+n_{j-1}+1,...,n_{1}+n_{2}+...+n_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}=\{n_{1}+n_{2}+...+n_{j-1}+1,...,n_{1}+n_{2}+...+n_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This serves to simplify the presentation of the argument.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Before giving the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, let us describe our approach by first constructing </span><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discontinuous</span><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text"> functions <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Of course, this is trivial to do, but the specific <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> that we construct will later be modified to obtain functions <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> which can be explicitly represented using deep ReLU networks and which will satisfy (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by partitioning the unit cube <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega:=[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega:=[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> sub-cubes of diameter at most <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/m</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we define</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}:=\frac{1}{m}\prod_{j=1}^{d}[\textbf{i}_{j},\textbf{i}_{j}+1),~\text{for $\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}$,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}:=\frac{1}{m}\prod_{j=1}^{d}[\textbf{i}_{j},\textbf{i}_{j}+1),~\text{for $\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">where if <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}_{j}=m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}_{j}=m-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then the half-open interval <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\textbf{i}_{j},\textbf{i}_{j}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\textbf{i}_{j},\textbf{i}_{j}+1)</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by the closed interval <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\textbf{i}_{j},\textbf{i}_{j}+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\textbf{i}_{j},\textbf{i}_{j}+1]</annotation></semantics></math> in the above product. The <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> sub-cubes <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint and cover the cube <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. We also denote by <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\textbf{i}}\in\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\textbf{i}}\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> the midpoint of the cube <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{\textbf{i}})_{j}=\frac{\textbf{i}_{j}+\frac{1}{2}}{m}~\text{for $j=1,...,d$}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{\textbf{i}})_{j}=\frac{\textbf{i}_{j}+\frac{1}{2}}{m}~\text{for $j=1,...,d$},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">and observe that for any <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x-z_{\textbf{i}}\|_{\infty}\leq 1/(2m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x-z_{\textbf{i}}\|_{\infty}\leq 1/(2m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=m^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=m^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We first construct a map <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the characteristic function of the sub-cube <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., we set</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x)=\begin{cases}1&amp;x\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}\\
0&amp;x\notin\Omega_{\textbf{i}}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x)=\begin{cases}1&amp;x\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}\\
0&amp;x\notin\Omega_{\textbf{i}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">We use the set <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to index the components of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the map <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Phi}(x)_{\textbf{i}}:=\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Phi}(x)_{\textbf{i}}:=\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">In other words, the <span id="S3.SS1.p9.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">i</span>-th component of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the characteristic function <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that if <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{i})_{i=1}^{n}\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{i})_{i=1}^{n}\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the inner sums in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) using the map <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\in\mathbb{Z}^{M}~\text{with}~\left[\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right]_{\textbf{i}}=|\{i\in P_{j}:x_{i}\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}\}|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\in\mathbb{Z}^{M}~\text{with}~\left[\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right]_{\textbf{i}}=|\{i\in P_{j}:x_{i}\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}\}|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">i.e., the <span id="S3.SS1.p10.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">i</span>-th component of this sum is equal to the number of coordinates <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> which lie in <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For an index <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}:=|P_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}:=|P_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{Z}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{Z}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\textbf{i}}\tau_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\textbf{i}}\tau_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> be a point <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\Omega^{n_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\Omega^{n_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> components of <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">i</span>. Such a <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to permutation and we choose <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> to be any such point. It is easy to see that for any <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{i})_{i=1}^{n_{j}}\in\Omega^{n_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{i})_{i=1}^{n_{j}}\in\Omega^{n_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a permutation <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{n_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{n_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\sigma(X)-Y\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n_{j}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)\right\|_{\infty}\leq\frac{1}{2m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\sigma(X)-Y\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n_{j}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)\right\|_{\infty}\leq\frac{1}{2m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, by reordering the points of <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> we can match each <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a corresponding <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> component of <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, since the number of <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> components of <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the number of <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s in <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now define the map <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}:\mathbb{R}^{M\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}:\mathbb{R}^{M\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})=f(Y(\tau_{1}),...,Y(\tau_{k}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})=f(Y(\tau_{1}),...,Y(\tau_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">for each <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. It then follows from the <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> invariance of <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> that (here <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by applying each of the permutations in (<a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) to the corresponding block <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</span></p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left|f(X)-\overline{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)\right|&amp;=\left|f(\sigma(X))-f\left(Y\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right),...,Y\left(\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)\right)\right|\\
&amp;\leq\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left|f(X)-\overline{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)\right|&amp;=\left|f(\sigma(X))-f\left(Y\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right),...,Y\left(\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)\right)\right|\\
&amp;\leq\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">We remark that our construction so far is similar to the construction given in Section 3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2022)</a></cite>. However, our construction is significantly simpler and results in a much smaller number of latent variables <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The next step is to construct <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> by reducing the embedding dimension from <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to one. We will do this by encoding the number of points in each cell using the digits of a large integer. Specifically, for <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</span></p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\textbf{i}|:=\sum_{j=1}^{d}\textbf{i}_{j}m^{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\textbf{i}|:=\sum_{j=1}^{d}\textbf{i}_{j}m^{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}(x)=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}}\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x)(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}(x)=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}}\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x)(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">In words, <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the integer whose expansion in base <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> contains a one in the position corresponding to the sub-cube <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> which contains <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and a zero in every other position. We observe that the inner sums in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) using <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\widetilde{\Phi}(x_{i})=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}}\left[\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x_{i})\right](n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\widetilde{\Phi}(x_{i})=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m-1\}^{d}}\left[\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x_{i})\right](n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since the number of terms in the inner sum is at most <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{j}|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{j}|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x_{i})\right]\leq n" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\chi_{\textbf{i}}(x_{i})\right]\leq n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.5.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the sum in (<a href="#S3.E25" title="Equation 25 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) is an integer whose digits in base <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> count the number of points <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which lie in the the sub-cube <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can now define <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> so that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}\left(\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{1})_{\textbf{i}}(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|},...,\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{k})_{\textbf{i}}(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}\right)=f(Y(\tau_{1}),...,Y(\tau_{k}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></munder><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></munder><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}\left(\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{1})_{\textbf{i}}(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|},...,\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{k})_{\textbf{i}}(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}\right)=f(Y(\tau_{1}),...,Y(\tau_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.6.1" class="ltx_text">for each <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})\in\mathbb{Z}^{M\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\textbf{i}}(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. It then follows that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\widetilde{\Phi}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\widetilde{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)=\overline{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\widetilde{\Phi}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\widetilde{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)=\overline{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\overline{\Phi}(x_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.7.1" class="ltx_text">and we have reduced the latent dimension to one.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The problem with the <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> which we have constructed is that <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is discontinuous (<math id="S3.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> could actually be taken continuous), and so it cannot even be approximated by ReLU neural networks. In order to prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we must construct maps <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> which are represented by ReLU networks and which satisfy (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), which is significantly more challenging. Our idea for achieving this is inspired by the proof of the Kolmorogov-Arnold superposition theorem (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kolmogorov (1957)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahane (1975)</a></cite>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first introduce the following partitions of unity on <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\delta&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\delta&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math> (which will be specified later) and consider the functions</span></p>
<table id="S3.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}(x):=\max\left\{0,\min\left\{\frac{1}{\delta}\left(\frac{1+\delta}{2}-\left|x-k\right|\right),1\right\}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}(x):=\max\left\{0,\min\left\{\frac{1}{\delta}\left(\frac{1+\delta}{2}-\left|x-k\right|\right),1\right\}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a simple matter to verify that the collection of functions <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{k}\}_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{k}\}_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is partition of unity on <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., that <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\phi_{k}(x)=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\phi_{k}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, each <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes outside the interval</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}:=\left(k-\frac{1+\delta}{2},k+\frac{1+\delta}{2}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}:=\left(k-\frac{1+\delta}{2},k+\frac{1+\delta}{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.4.1" class="ltx_text">and <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> on the interval</span></p>
<table id="S3.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{I}_{k}:=\left[k-\frac{1-\delta}{2},k+\frac{1-\delta}{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{I}_{k}:=\left[k-\frac{1-\delta}{2},k+\frac{1-\delta}{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.5.1" class="ltx_text">It is also clear from (<a href="#S3.E29" title="Equation 29 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) that each of the functions <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed using a ReLU neural network with width <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the partition of unity <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{k}\}_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{k}\}_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> we can obtain a partition of unity on <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> via a tensor product construction. Specifically, for an index <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we set</span></p>
<table id="S3.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textbf{i}}(x):=\prod_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textbf{i}}(x):=\prod_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then the functions <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> form a partition of unity on <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the integer <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we would like to consider the scaled partition of unity</span></p>
<table id="S3.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textbf{i},m}(x):=\phi_{\textbf{i}}(mx)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textbf{i},m}(x):=\phi_{\textbf{i}}(mx)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p16.3.1" class="ltx_text">restricted to the unit cube <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that exactly <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of the functions <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textbf{i},m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textbf{i},m}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-zero on <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> (namely those for which <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m\}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\{0,...,m\}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>). We would like these <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> functions to be continuous substitutes for the characteristic functions <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> in our discontinuous construction (note we have slightly redefined the variable <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>). However, the overlap in their supports means that the resulting map <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> would no longer map into <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, but rather into a much more complicated <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional cell complex in <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. We would have to control the map <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> on this set which is difficult to do using ReLU networks and introduces significant technical complications.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our method for overcoming this is to instead use multiple shifted partitions of unity, in a manner similar to the proof of the Kolmogorov-Arnold superposition theorem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Specifically, we set <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\frac{1}{2dn+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\frac{1}{2dn+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the following collection of <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2dn+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2dn+1</annotation></semantics></math> shifted partitions of unity. For <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0,...,2dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0,...,2dn</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we set (here <span id="S3.SS1.p18.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">1</span> denotes the vector of ones)</span></p>
<table id="S3.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}(x):=\phi_{\textbf{i}}(x-q\delta\textbf{1})=\prod_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}(x):=\phi_{\textbf{i}}(x-q\delta\textbf{1})=\prod_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">We then write <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}:=\{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}},~\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}:=\{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}},~\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-th shifted partition of unity.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">For a given point <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is </span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bad</span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.3" class="ltx_text"> for <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists an <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}(x)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}(x)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, we say that the partition is </span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">good</span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.5" class="ltx_text"> for <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. For a point <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}</annotation></semantics></math> we say that the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is </span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bad</span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.7" class="ltx_text"> for <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if it is bad for any coordinate <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and </span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">good</span><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.9" class="ltx_text"> if it is good for all coordinates <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">The key observation, inspired by the proof of the Kolmogorov-Arnold theorem, is that for each point <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of partitions <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> which are bad for <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows since if <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is bad for <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> then there must be an index <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,...,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,...,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> and an index <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-q\delta\in I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-q\delta\in I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, otherwise every factor on the right hand side in (<a href="#S3.E33" title="Equation 33 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) would be either <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the product would be either <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}=\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}\left(k+\frac{1-\delta}{2},k+\frac{1+\delta}{2}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}=\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}\left(k+\frac{1-\delta}{2},k+\frac{1+\delta}{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text">and since <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\frac{1}{2dn+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\frac{1}{2dn+1}</annotation></semantics></math> it follows that the sets</span></p>
<table id="S3.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{q}:=q\delta+\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{q}:=q\delta+\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p20.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p20.3.1" class="ltx_text">are disjoint for <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0,...,2dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0,...,2dn</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that for each <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> there is at most one value of <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}\in B_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}\in B_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., such that <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-q\delta\in I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-q\delta\in I_{k}\backslash\tilde{I}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, there is at most one bad value of <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> for each index <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, and so at most <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in total. We deduce from this that for any point <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of bad partitions is at most <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dn</annotation></semantics></math>, since each coordinate <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has at most <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> bad partitions. Since the number of partitions is <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2dn+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2dn+1</annotation></semantics></math>, this implies that for each <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}</annotation></semantics></math> only a minority of the partitions <math id="S3.SS1.p20.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are bad.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we modify our partition of unity. We define</span></p>
<table id="S3.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x):=\max\left\{0,\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)-d+1\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x):=\max\left\{0,\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)-d+1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p21.2.1" class="ltx_text">Although these functions no longer form a partition of unity, we still have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p21.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p21.3.1" class="ltx_text">whenever the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, then either all terms in the product in (<a href="#S3.E33" title="Equation 33 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) are <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or one of them is <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. In the first case, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)-d+1=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)-d+1=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p21.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p21.4.1" class="ltx_text">so that <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, while in the second case we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)-d+1\leq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\textbf{i}_{j}}(x_{j}-q\delta)-d+1\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p21.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p21.5.1" class="ltx_text">so that <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, we see from this argument that if <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textbf{i}}^{q}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textbf{i}}^{q}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then also <math id="S3.SS1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using these observations, we construct the network <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. For <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we denote by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x):=\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(mx)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x):=\widetilde{\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i}}}(mx).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we set</span></p>
<table id="S3.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q,m}=\mathbb{Z}\cap\left(-\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta,~m+\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q,m}=\mathbb{Z}\cap\left(-\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta,~m+\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p22.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p22.3.1" class="ltx_text">and write</span></p>
<table id="S3.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{q,m}=\prod_{j=1}^{d}I_{q,m}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{q,m}=\prod_{j=1}^{d}I_{q,m}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p22.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p22.4.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that if <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\notin I_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\notin I_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I_{k}+q\delta)\cap[0,m]=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I_{k}+q\delta)\cap[0,m]=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}(m(x-q\delta))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}(m(x-q\delta))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, if any component <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}_{j}\notin I_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}_{j}\notin I_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., if <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\notin\mathcal{I}_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\notin\mathcal{I}_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textbf{i},m}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textbf{i},m}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">We claim that for each <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I_{q,m}|=(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I_{q,m}|=(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{I}_{q,m}|=(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{I}_{q,m}|=(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the length of the interval</span></p>
<table id="S3.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(-\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta,~m+\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(-\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta,~m+\frac{1+\delta}{2}-q\delta\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p23.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p23.2.1" class="ltx_text">is <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1+\delta&gt;m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1+\delta&gt;m+1</annotation></semantics></math> it must contain at least <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math> integers. To show that it always contains exactly <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math> integers, we note that for <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=d</annotation></semantics></math> the interval starts at <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and thus contains exactly <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math> integers. Since all other intervals are shifts of this by a multiple of <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the length of these open intervals is <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that all shifts also contain exactly <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math> integers.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">We write</span></p>
<table id="S3.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{q,m}(x):=(\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x))_{\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}}\in\mathbb{R}^{(m+1)^{d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{q,m}(x):=(\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x))_{\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}}\in\mathbb{R}^{(m+1)^{d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p24.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p24.2.1" class="ltx_text">These functions can be expressed using ReLU networks as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Each of the functions <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}(m(x_{j}-q\delta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}(m(x_{j}-q\delta))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0,...,2dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0,...,2dn</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,...,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,...,d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in I_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in I_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed using a ReLU network of width <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math> (see (<a href="#S3.E29" title="Equation 29 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>)). Hence a network whose output consists of all <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> of these functions can be represented by a ReLU network of width <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2d(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2d(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math>. By adding a final layer of width <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (with an application of ReLU at the end), the function <math id="S3.SS1.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented (see (<a href="#S3.E36" title="Equation 36 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>)).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">The next step is to reduce the embedding dimension using the digit encoding described previously in (<a href="#S3.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>). To this end, we add a linear layer to the end of each of these networks to obtain functions</span></p>
<table id="S3.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{q}(x):=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}}\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x)(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}\in\mathbb{R}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{q}(x):=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}}\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x)(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}\in\mathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">where in this case <math id="S3.SS1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\textbf{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\textbf{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the index of <span id="S3.SS1.p26.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">i</span> in some (fixed) enumeration of the set <math id="S3.SS1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of these functions can be represented by a ReLU network with three hidden layers of width <math id="S3.SS1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2d(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2d(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and one hidden layer with width <math id="S3.SS1.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now define the function <math id="S3.SS1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)=\begin{pmatrix}\Phi_{0}(x)\\
\Phi_{1}(x)\\
\vdots\\
\Phi_{2dn}(x)\end{pmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{2dn+1}=\mathbb{R}^{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)=\begin{pmatrix}\Phi_{0}(x)\\
\Phi_{1}(x)\\
\vdots\\
\Phi_{2dn}(x)\end{pmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{2dn+1}=\mathbb{R}^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p27.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p27.2.1" class="ltx_text">By concatenating the networks representing each <math id="S3.SS1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, this function can be expressed using a ReLU network with three hidden layers of width <math id="S3.SS1.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2d(m+1)(2dn+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2d(m+1)(2dn+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and one hidden layer of width <math id="S3.SS1.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2dn+1)(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2dn+1)(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we describe how to construct the network <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. We first note that by adjusting the bias on the output, we may assume without loss of generality that the value of <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at the midpoint of <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (namely at the point where every entry is <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) is equal to <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. It then follows that for any <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(X)|\leq\omega(f,1/2)\leq m\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(X)|\leq\omega(f,1/2)\leq m\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p28.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p28.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider a single partition <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0,...,2dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0,...,2dn</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math> we consider the rectangle</span></p>
<table id="S3.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}:=\left(q\delta\textbf{1}+\frac{1}{m}\prod_{j=1}^{d}\tilde{I}_{\textbf{i}_{j}}\right)\cap\Omega," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}:=\left(q\delta\textbf{1}+\frac{1}{m}\prod_{j=1}^{d}\tilde{I}_{\textbf{i}_{j}}\right)\cap\Omega,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p28.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p28.3.1" class="ltx_text">and we denote by <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\textbf{i},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\textbf{i},q}</annotation></semantics></math> the center point of this rectangle. Note that for each point <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x-z_{\textbf{i},q}\|_{\infty}\leq 1/2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x-z_{\textbf{i},q}\|_{\infty}\leq 1/2m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for an input <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q,j}(X):=\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\Phi_{q}(x_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q,j}(X):=\sum_{i\in P_{j}}\Phi_{q}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p29.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p29.2.1" class="ltx_text">is an integer of the form</span></p>
<table id="S3.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q,j}(X)=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}}\tau_{q,j}(X)_{\textbf{i}}(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q,j}(X)=\sum_{\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}}\tau_{q,j}(X)_{\textbf{i}}(n+1)^{|\textbf{i}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p29.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p29.3.1" class="ltx_text">whose digits in base <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q,j}(X)_{\textbf{i}}=\left|\left\{i\in P_{j}:~x_{i}\in\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}\right\}\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q,j}(X)_{\textbf{i}}=\left|\left\{i\in P_{j}:~x_{i}\in\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}\right\}\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p29.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p29.4.1" class="ltx_text">This holds since if <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then by construction <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,...,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,...,n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{q,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{q}_{\textbf{i},m}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> iff</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-q\delta\textbf{1}\in\frac{1}{m}\prod_{j=1}^{d}\tilde{I}_{\textbf{i}_{j}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-q\delta\textbf{1}\in\frac{1}{m}\prod_{j=1}^{d}\tilde{I}_{\textbf{i}_{j}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p29.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p29.5.1" class="ltx_text">Since the total number of points in each rectangle is at most <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the digits in base <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> add without carrying when summing the <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{q}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{q}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first step in the network <math id="S3.SS1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is to extract the digits <math id="S3.SS1.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q,j}(X)_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q,j}(X)_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be done using a well-known construction with a ‘bit-extraction’ network of width <math id="S3.SS1.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=C(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=C(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.SS1.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=C(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=C(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for an absolute constant <math id="S3.SS1.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartlett et al. (1998)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2018)</a></cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite>, Proposition 10).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, given an integer vector <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{Z}^{(m+1)^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{Z}^{(m+1)^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\textbf{i}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\textbf{i}}\tau_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\textbf{i}}\tau_{\textbf{i}}=n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (recall that <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}:=|P_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}:=|P_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math>), we let <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{q}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{q}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> be a point <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\Omega^{n_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\Omega^{n_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> components of <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\textbf{i},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\textbf{i},q}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <span id="S3.SS1.p31.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">i</span>. Such a <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to permutation of the indices in <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and we choose <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{q}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{q}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> to be any such point. We set <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon:=\omega(f,1/2m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon:=\omega(f,1/2m)</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}\in\mathbb{Z}^{(m+1)^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}\in\mathbb{Z}^{(m+1)^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> we define</span></p>
<table id="S3.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(\tau):=\epsilon\left\lfloor\frac{f(Y_{q}(\tau_{1}),...,Y_{q}(\tau_{k}))}{\epsilon}\right\rfloor," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(\tau):=\epsilon\left\lfloor\frac{f(Y_{q}(\tau_{1}),...,Y_{q}(\tau_{k}))}{\epsilon}\right\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p31.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p31.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the floor is rounded towards <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(\tau_{1},...,\tau_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}\in\mathbb{Z}^{(m+1)^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}\in\mathbb{Z}^{(m+1)^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> we say that the set <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> realizes <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a point in <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in K</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{j})_{\textbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> of its coordinates in the block <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the rectangle <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the distance between the rectangles <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{j},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">j</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{j},q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{j}\neq\textbf{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">j</mtext><mo>≠</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{j}\neq\textbf{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/m</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that the points in <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> realizing two different <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>’s must be at least distance <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/m</annotation></semantics></math> (even modulo the action of <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence the number of <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>’s which are realized by <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p32.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\delta/(2m)=\frac{1}{2m(2nd+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\delta/(2m)=\frac{1}{2m(2nd+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">We define a function <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}_{q}:\mathbb{R}^{(m+1)^{d}\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}_{q}:\mathbb{R}^{(m+1)^{d}\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}_{q}(\tau)=\bar{f}(\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}_{q}(\tau)=\bar{f}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p33.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p33.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> which are realized by <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an interpolation problem and we can use the results of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vardi et al. (2022)</a></cite> to construct a ReLU network satisfying (<a href="#S3.E53" title="Equation 53 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>). Specifically, the number of integer vectors is bounded by <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>,
any two such integer vectors are at least distance one apart, and each of them has length at most <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, using equation (<a href="#S3.E47" title="Equation 47 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) we see that the number of possible values for <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we can apply Theorem 3.1 from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vardi et al. (2022)</a></cite> to see that this interpolation problem can be solved by a ReLU network with width <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=12</annotation></semantics></math> and depth</span></p>
<table id="S3.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq C\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log(n(2m+1)N_{r}(K))}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq C\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log(n(2m+1)N_{r}(K))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p33.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p33.3.1" class="ltx_text">for an absolute constant <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. We then construct <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> by composing this network with the bit-extractor network described above.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now observe that if the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in K</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|f(X)-\rho_{q}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi_{q}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi_{q}(x_{i})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|f(X)-\rho_{q}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi_{q}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi_{q}(x_{i})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p34.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p34.2.1" class="ltx_text">This holds since by construction there exists a permutation <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\sigma(X)-Y_{q}(\tau(X))|_{\infty}\leq 1/2m," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\sigma(X)-Y_{q}(\tau(X))|_{\infty}\leq 1/2m,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p34.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p34.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(X)=(\tau_{1}(X),...,\tau_{k}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(X)=(\tau_{1}(X),...,\tau_{k}(X))</annotation></semantics></math> is the integer vector giving the number of coordinates in the block <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> that lie in the rectangle <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">i</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\textbf{i},q}</annotation></semantics></math>
(see the remark following (<a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) and piece together the permutations in each block). By (<a href="#S3.E52" title="Equation 52 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\bar{f}(\tau)-f(Y_{q}(\tau))|\leq\epsilon=\omega(f,1/2m)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\bar{f}(\tau)-f(Y_{q}(\tau))|\leq\epsilon=\omega(f,1/2m),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p34.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p34.4.1" class="ltx_text">so that by the <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariance of <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left|f(X)-\tilde{\rho}_{q}\left(\tau(X)\right)\right|&amp;\leq|f(\sigma(X))-f(Y_{q}(\tau(X))|\\
&amp;+|f(Y_{q}((X))-\bar{f}(\tau(X))|\\
&amp;\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left|f(X)-\tilde{\rho}_{q}\left(\tau(X)\right)\right|&amp;\leq|f(\sigma(X))-f(Y_{q}(\tau(X))|\\
&amp;+|f(Y_{q}((X))-\bar{f}(\tau(X))|\\
&amp;\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p34.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p34.5.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, if <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is bad for <math id="S3.SS1.p34.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have no control on the error in (<a href="#S3.E55" title="Equation 55 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now obtain a new network by applying each <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> to the corresponding input in parallel, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}\begin{pmatrix}\tau_{0}\\
\tau_{1}\\
\tau_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\tau_{2dn}\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{0}(\tau_{0})\\
\rho_{1}(\tau_{1})\\
\rho_{2}(\tau_{2})\\
\vdots\\
\rho_{2dn}(\tau_{2dn})\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}\begin{pmatrix}\tau_{0}\\
\tau_{1}\\
\tau_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\tau_{2dn}\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{0}(\tau_{0})\\
\rho_{1}(\tau_{1})\\
\rho_{2}(\tau_{2})\\
\vdots\\
\rho_{2dn}(\tau_{2dn})\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p35.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p35.2.1" class="ltx_text">By concatenating the constructions of each <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, this can be done using a network with <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> layers of width <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(n+1)(2nd+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(n+1)(2nd+1)</annotation></semantics></math> followed by network with width <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=12(2dn+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=12(2dn+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> bounded (<a href="#S3.E54" title="Equation 54 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>). The final network <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by taking the median of the outputs of <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Corollary 13 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite> this can be done by increasing the depth of the network by <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\log(2dn+1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\log(2dn+1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we prove that (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) holds. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by construction</span></p>
<table id="S3.E60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi(x_{i})\right)=\left[\rho_{q}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi_{q,m}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi_{q,m}(x_{i})\right)\right]_{q=0}^{2dn}\in\mathbb{R}^{2dn+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\rho}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi(x_{i})\right)=\left[\rho_{q}\left(\sum_{i\in P_{1}}\Phi_{q,m}(x_{i}),...,\sum_{i\in P_{k}}\Phi_{q,m}(x_{i})\right)\right]_{q=0}^{2dn}\in\mathbb{R}^{2dn+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(60)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p36.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p36.2.1" class="ltx_text">If the partition <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is good for <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then the bound (<a href="#S3.E55" title="Equation 55 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) holds. Since the majority of the partitions <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are good for any given <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, this means that the majority of the coordinates in (<a href="#S3.E60" title="Equation 60 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">60</span></a>) satisfy the bound (<a href="#S3.E55" title="Equation 55 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>). Consequently, the median must be sandwiched between two of these good coordinates, and thus must also satisfy the bound (<a href="#S3.E55" title="Equation 55 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>). Since <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be this median, this proves that (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Universal representations of group-<em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">equivariant</em> functions on point clouds have yielded both theoretical and practical benefits in geometric machine learning research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Villar et al. (2021)</a></cite>. It allows researchers to verify that their model respects the underlying symmetries of the data. A method to extend a universal permutation <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invariant</em> deepset model to a universal permutation <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">equivariant</em> model was discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Segol and Lipman (2020)</a></cite> and later in the similar Sumformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite>. We derive, for the first time, approximation rates for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous permutation-equivariant functions and apply these results to obtain approximation rates for the Sumformer architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first present a Deep Sets-like representation for purely permutation <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equivariant</span> functions that accounts for the regularity of each component in the decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[Characterization of Permutation-Equivariant Functions]
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous function that is equivariant to permutations. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)_{i}=g(x_{i},\;\{x_{j}\mid j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\setminus\{i\}\;\})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)_{i}=g(x_{i},\;\{x_{j}\mid j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\setminus\{i\}\;\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Part I - Proof of Existence of Shared Function.</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"> We use the equivariance property to derive symmetry conditions on the entry-wise functions of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. By the equivariance constraint, for any permutation <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\sigma X)=\sigma f(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\sigma X)=\sigma f(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1}(\sigma X),\ldots,f_{n}(\sigma X))=(f_{\sigma(1)}(X),\ldots,f_{\sigma(n)}(X))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1}(\sigma X),\ldots,f_{n}(\sigma X))=(f_{\sigma(1)}(X),\ldots,f_{\sigma(n)}(X))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the equality, for any <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\sigma(X))=f_{\sigma(i)}(X)\underset{j=\sigma(i)}{\implies}f_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(\sigma(X))=f_{j}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\sigma(X))=f_{\sigma(i)}(X)\underset{j=\sigma(i)}{\implies}f_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(\sigma(X))=f_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Averaging over all permutations,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}(X)=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{n}}f_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(\sigma X)=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(j),\sigma_{0}=(ij)}f_{i}(\sigma\sigma_{0}X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}(X)=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{n}}f_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(\sigma X)=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(j),\sigma_{0}=(ij)}f_{i}(\sigma\sigma_{0}X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(j),\sigma_{0}=(kj),\sigma_{1}=(ki)}f_{(\sigma\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1})^{-1}(j)}(\sigma\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1}X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(j),\sigma_{0}=(kj),\sigma_{1}=(ki)}f_{(\sigma\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1})^{-1}(j)}(\sigma\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1}X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(j),\sigma_{0}=(kj),\sigma_{1}=(ki)}f_{i}(\sigma\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1}X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(j),\sigma_{0}=(kj),\sigma_{1}=(ki)}f_{i}(\sigma\sigma_{0}\sigma_{1}X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(k),\sigma_{0}=(kj)}f_{(i)}(\sigma\sigma_{0}X)=f_{k}((jk)\cdot X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\frac{1}{|S_{n}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\sigma\in Stab(k),\sigma_{0}=(kj)}f_{(i)}(\sigma\sigma_{0}X)=f_{k}((jk)\cdot X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">for any <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Denote <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X):=f_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X):=f_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(\sigma(X))=f_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(\sigma(X))=f_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have for any <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in Stab(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in Stab(j)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}(X)=f_{j}(\sigma X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}(X)=f_{j}(\sigma X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g(X)=g(x_{j},\{x_{i}\mid i=1,\ldots,n\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)=g(x_{j},\{x_{i}\mid i=1,\ldots,n\})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}(X)=f_{j}((jk)\cdot X)=g(x_{k},\{x_{i}\mid i\in[n]\setminus\{j\}\})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}(X)=f_{j}((jk)\cdot X)=g(x_{k},\{x_{i}\mid i\in[n]\setminus\{j\}\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Part II - Proof of Regularity.</span><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text">
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hölder exponent and assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous, that is, for every <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f(X)-f(Y)\|_{2}\leq C\|X-Y\|_{2}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f(X)-f(Y)\|_{2}\leq C\|X-Y\|_{2}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
denote <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(i)}=(x_{i},x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{i-1},x_{i+1},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(i)}=(x_{i},x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{i-1},x_{i+1},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the reordering of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th component appearing first and then the remaining components in order, where a permutation <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\cdot X^{(i)}=(x_{\sigma(i)},x_{\sigma(1)},\ldots,x_{\sigma(i-1)},x_{\sigma(i+1)},\ldots,x_{\sigma(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\cdot X^{(i)}=(x_{\sigma(i)},x_{\sigma(1)},\ldots,x_{\sigma(i-1)},x_{\sigma(i+1)},\ldots,x_{\sigma(n)})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(X)=g(X^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(X)=g(X^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f_{i}(X)-f_{i}(Y)\|_{2}\leq\|f(X)-f(Y)\|_{2}\leq C\|X-Y\|^{\alpha}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f_{i}(X)-f_{i}(Y)\|_{2}\leq\|f(X)-f(Y)\|_{2}\leq C\|X-Y\|^{\alpha}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, this directly gives <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder . Therefore, for each index <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, taking the minimum over Stab<math id="S3.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> permutations we have</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g(X^{(i)})-g(Y^{(i)})\|_{2}^{2}=\underset{\pi\in Stab(i)}{\min}\|g(X^{(i)})-g(\pi\cdot Y^{(i)})\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g(X^{(i)})-g(Y^{(i)})\|_{2}^{2}=\underset{\pi\in Stab(i)}{\min}\|g(X^{(i)})-g(\pi\cdot Y^{(i)})\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\underset{\pi\in Stab(i)}{\min}\|f(X)_{i}-f(\pi Y)_{i}\|_{2}^{2}\leq C\underset{\pi\in Stab(i)}{\min}\|X-\pi Y\|_{2}^{2\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\underset{\pi\in Stab(i)}{\min}\|f(X)_{i}-f(\pi Y)_{i}\|_{2}^{2}\leq C\underset{\pi\in Stab(i)}{\min}\|X-\pi Y\|_{2}^{2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore if <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with a constant of <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with the same constant with respect to the <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Stab(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Stab(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant metric on <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the other direction, assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with a constant of <math id="S3.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the Stab<math id="S3.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant norm, that is</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g(X)-g(Y)\|_{2}\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}C\underset{\pi\in\Pi(1)}{\min}\|X-\pi Y\|_{2}^{\alpha},\;\forall X,Y" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g(X)-g(Y)\|_{2}\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}C\underset{\pi\in\Pi(1)}{\min}\|X-\pi Y\|_{2}^{\alpha},\;\forall X,Y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(61)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then it holds that</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f(X)-f(Y)\|_{2}^{2}=\|\left(g(X^{(1)}),g(X^{(2)}),\ldots,g(X^{(n)})\right)-\left(g(Y^{(1)}),g(Y^{(2)}),\ldots,g(Y^{(n)})\right)\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f(X)-f(Y)\|_{2}^{2}=\|\left(g(X^{(1)}),g(X^{(2)}),\ldots,g(X^{(n)})\right)-\left(g(Y^{(1)}),g(Y^{(2)}),\ldots,g(Y^{(n)})\right)\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|g(X^{(i)})-g(Y^{(i)})\|_{2}^{2}\underset{\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:stab-lipchitz}}{\leq}\frac{1}{n}C^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\underset{\pi\in\Pi(1)}{\min}\|(1\;i)X-\pi(1\;i)Y\|_{2}^{2\alpha}\leq C^{2}\|X-Y\|_{2}^{2\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>≤</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S3.E61" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">61</span></a></mtext></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|g(X^{(i)})-g(Y^{(i)})\|_{2}^{2}\underset{\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:stab-lipchitz}}{\leq}\frac{1}{n}C^{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\underset{\pi\in\Pi(1)}{\min}\|(1\;i)X-\pi(1\;i)Y\|_{2}^{2\alpha}\leq C^{2}\|X-Y\|_{2}^{2\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">as each element in the average in smaller than the quantity on the right-hand side of the inequality. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder as well.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">As a consequence of Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we obtain approximation rates for <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous permutation-equivariant functions.
<span id="S3.SS2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>[Universal Approximation of Permutation Equivariant Functions]
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose that <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant function. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer and set <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2dn+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2dn+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exist ReLU neural networks <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following structure:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> has three hidden layers of width <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2d(2dn+1)(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2d(2dn+1)(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by one hidden layer of width <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an output layer of width <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2nd+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2nd+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> layers of width <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> followed by <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers of width <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=12(2dn+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=12(2dn+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{1}{2m(2nd+1)},\quad L\leq C\max\{\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log[n(2m+1)N_{r}(K)]},\log(2nd+1)^{2}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{1}{2m(2nd+1)},\quad L\leq C\max\{\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log[n(2m+1)N_{r}(K)]},\log(2nd+1)^{2}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an absolute constant.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.p19.2" class="ltx_p">such that</p>
<table id="S3.E62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|f(X)_{i}-\rho\left(\Phi(x_{i}),\sum_{j\in[n]\setminus\{i\}}\Phi(x_{j})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|f(X)_{i}-\rho\left(\Phi(x_{i}),\sum_{j\in[n]\setminus\{i\}}\Phi(x_{j})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(62)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p19.3" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, each entry <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of an <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder continuous, permutation equivariant function is defined by a shared <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder invariant function corresponding to a partition <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=\{i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=\{i\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}=[n]\setminus\{i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}=[n]\setminus\{i\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We immediately obtain the desired result by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p">In the next subsection, we explore whether the recently popular Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite> architecture can approximate continuous equivariant functions with the same approximation rates as Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. We first detail the mechanics of the Transformer architecture and then proceed to derive the desired approximation rates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Definition of the Transformer Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first define the components of the Transformer architecture, as referenced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 0</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Attention Head (Def. 2.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite>))<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be weight matrices. A </span><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">self-attention head</span><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a function <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{AttHead}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{AttHead}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></p>
<table id="S3.E63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{AttHead}(X):=\mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{(XW_{Q})(XW_{K})^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)XW_{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{AttHead}(X):=\mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{(XW_{Q})(XW_{K})^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)XW_{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(63)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softmax}</annotation></semantics></math> is the softmax function applied row-wise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 0</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Attention Layer (Def. 2.2 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite>))<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{AttHead}_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{AttHead}_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{AttHead}_{2},...,\textnormal{AttHead}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{AttHead}_{2},...,\textnormal{AttHead}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> be attention heads, and
let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{hd\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{hd\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. A </span><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(multi-head) self-attention layer</span><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{Att}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Att</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{Att}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{Att}(X):=[\textnormal{AttHead}_{1}(X),...,\textnormal{AttHead}_{h}(X)]W_{O}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Att</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{Att}(X):=[\textnormal{AttHead}_{1}(X),...,\textnormal{AttHead}_{h}(X)]W_{O}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(64)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 0</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer Block (Def. 2.3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite>))<span id="Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A </span><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformer</span><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">block</span><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{Block}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Block</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{Block}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an attention layer <span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">Att</span> followed by a fully-connected feed forward layer <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FC:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FC:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with </span><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">residual connections</span><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{Block}(X):=X+FC(X+\textnormal{Att}(X))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Block</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>Att</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{Block}(X):=X+FC(X+\textnormal{Att}(X))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(65)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FC</annotation></semantics></math> is applied row-wise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 0</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer Network (Def. 2.4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite>))<span id="Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{Block}_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Block</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{Block}_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{Block}_{2},...,\textnormal{Block}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Block</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>Block</mtext><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{Block}_{2},...,\textnormal{Block}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> be Transformer blocks. A </span><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformer network</span><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a composition of Transformer blocks:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(X):=(\textnormal{Block}_{l}\circ\dots\circ\textnormal{Block}_{1})(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Block</mtext><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mtext>Block</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(X):=(\textnormal{Block}_{l}\circ\dots\circ\textnormal{Block}_{1})(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(66)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using the definitions above, we prove the Transformer architecture can approximate permutation equivariant functions with identical approximation rates as in Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite> Approximation Rates]
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equivariant function. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer and set <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(2dn+1)(m+1)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(2dn+1)(m+1)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a 2-Block Transformer network <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the fully-connected layers (see Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 0 (Transformer Block (Def. 2.3 in ( ) )). ‣ 3.3 Definition of the Transformer Architecture ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) satisfying <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FC_{1}=\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FC_{1}=\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FC_{2}=\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FC_{2}=\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> are ReLU Neural Networks which satisfy the conditions of Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, up to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> having an additional linear layer of width <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N+d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N+d+1</annotation></semantics></math> and up to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> being preceded by a linear projection from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N+d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N+d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math> that extracts the last <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates, such that</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|f(X)_{i}-\mathcal{T}(X)_{i}\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|f(X)_{i}-\mathcal{T}(X)_{i}\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(67)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Definition of the Transformer Architecture ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now show that a Transformer Network (Definition 4) can be constructed to compute the expression <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\left(\Phi(x_{i}),\sum_{j\in[n]\setminus\{i\}}\Phi(x_{j})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\left(\Phi(x_{i}),\sum_{j\in[n]\setminus\{i\}}\Phi(x_{j})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> from Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. We follow the constructive proof from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberti et al. (2023)</a></cite> (Appendix B), modifying it to compute the ‘omit-one’ sum directly. The target expression is <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i}=\sum_{j\neq i}\Phi(x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i}=\sum_{j\neq i}\Phi(x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by leveraging the feed-forward network <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, guaranteed to exist by Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. We modify <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> by enlarging its width by <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math> so that the identity <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a constant <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> are passed through alongside the original output. We then append a final linear layer that produces both <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Phi(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Phi(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the augmented map <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Phi}(x)=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),-\Phi(x_{i})]\in\mathbb{R}^{1+d+2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Phi}(x)=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),-\Phi(x_{i})]\in\mathbb{R}^{1+d+2N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We then form an augmented matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(1+d+2N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(1+d+2N)}</annotation></semantics></math> where the <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1})_{i}=\hat{\Phi}(x_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1})_{i}=\hat{\Phi}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">This <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the input to the Transformer Block (Definition 3). The block’s first operation is <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}+\text{Att}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>Att</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}+\text{Att}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. We configure one Attention Head (Definition 1) to compute the global sum <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\Phi}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\Phi}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=1+d+2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=1+d+2N</annotation></semantics></math> be the dimension of the augmented space. We set the attention head’s key/query dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be the first standard basis vector. We define the query and key matrices <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}=W_{K}=e_{1}=[1,0,\dots,0]^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}=W_{K}=e_{1}=[1,0,\dots,0]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">The resulting query and key matrices are <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=X_{1}W_{Q}=\bm{1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=X_{1}W_{Q}=\bm{1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=X_{1}W_{K}=\bm{1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=X_{1}W_{K}=\bm{1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The attention matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is then:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathrm{softmax}(QK^{T}/\sqrt{d})=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{1}_{n}\bm{1}_{n}^{T}/\sqrt{d})=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{1}_{n\times n}/\sqrt{d})=\frac{1}{n}\bm{1}_{n\times n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathrm{softmax}(QK^{T}/\sqrt{d})=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{1}_{n}\bm{1}_{n}^{T}/\sqrt{d})=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{1}_{n\times n}/\sqrt{d})=\frac{1}{n}\bm{1}_{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softmax}</annotation></semantics></math> is the row-wise softmax function.
We define the value matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> using a block structure based on the partition <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(1+d)+N+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(1+d)+N+N</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}_{(1+d)\times(1+d+N)}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{(1+d)\times N}\\
\mathbf{0}_{N\times(1+d+N)}&amp;n\cdot I_{N}\\
\mathbf{0}_{N\times(1+d+N)}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{N\times N}\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}_{(1+d)\times(1+d+N)}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{(1+d)\times N}\\
\mathbf{0}_{N\times(1+d+N)}&amp;n\cdot I_{N}\\
\mathbf{0}_{N\times(1+d+N)}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{N\times N}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">The row-wise application <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1}W_{V})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1}W_{V})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> selects the <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> components (from the third block of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), scales them by <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and places them in the final <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> columns.
The output of the attention head <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AttHead}(X_{1})=A(X_{1}W_{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AttHead}(X_{1})=A(X_{1}W_{V})</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{AttHead}(X_{1}))_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}(X_{1}W_{V})_{j}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}[\mathbf{0}_{1+d+N},n\cdot\Phi(x_{j})]=[\mathbf{0}_{1+d+N},\Sigma_{\Phi}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{AttHead}(X_{1}))_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}(X_{1}W_{V})_{j}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}[\mathbf{0}_{1+d+N},n\cdot\Phi(x_{j})]=[\mathbf{0}_{1+d+N},\Sigma_{\Phi}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\Phi}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\Phi}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\Phi(x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Transformer Block (Definition 3) then adds the residual <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to this attention output. For the <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row, this operation yields:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(X_{1}+\text{AttHead}(X_{1}))_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(X_{1}+\text{AttHead}(X_{1}))_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(X_{1})_{i}+(\text{AttHead}(X_{1}))_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>AttHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(X_{1})_{i}+(\text{AttHead}(X_{1}))_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),-\Phi(x_{i})]+[\mathbf{0}_{1+d+N},\Sigma_{\Phi}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),-\Phi(x_{i})]+[\mathbf{0}_{1+d+N},\Sigma_{\Phi}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.6.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}=[1,x_{i},\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math> be this resulting vector. This vector <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains both the ‘self’ embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and the ‘omit-one’ sum <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i}=\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i}=\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we apply a feed-forward network <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> (with the specified width and depth) that takes <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as input. After projecting <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> naturally to the last <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates, <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2N}(Z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2N}(Z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which now contains both <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\Phi}^{\setminus i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we plug into <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the transformer can express the target function <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\Phi(x_{i}),\Sigma_{\Phi}-\Phi(x_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>. This is precisely the expression from Corollary <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, Equation (<a href="#S3.E62" title="Equation 62 ‣ 3.2 Approximation Rates for Permutation Equivariant Functions: Sumformer ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">62</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> also gives us a new approximation result for approximation of non-invariant functions. A non-invariant function <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be seen as an <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant function in the special case where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\left\{\{1\},\{2\},\ldots,\{n\}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\left\{\{1\},\{2\},\ldots,\{n\}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Setting <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we obtain the following corollary
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
Let <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,1]^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,1]^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\Omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\Omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> the smallest number of balls (in the <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm) of radius <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> required to cover <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer and set <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exist ReLU neural networks <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}^{N\times k}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfyfing that for every <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},...,x_{n})\in K</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|f(X)-\rho\left(\Phi(x_{1}),...,\Phi(x_{n})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|f(X)-\rho\left(\Phi(x_{1}),...,\Phi(x_{n})\right)\right|\leq 2\omega\left(f,\frac{1}{2m}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(68)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> has three hidden layers of width <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=2(2n+1)(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=2(2n+1)(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by one hidden layer of width <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and an output layer of width <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> layers of width <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> followed by <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers of width <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=12(2n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=12(2n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{1}{2m(2n+1)},\quad L\leq C\max\{\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log[n(2m+1)N_{r}(K)]},\log(2nd+1)^{2}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{1}{2m(2n+1)},\quad L\leq C\max\{\sqrt{N_{r}(K)\log[n(2m+1)N_{r}(K)]},\log(2nd+1)^{2}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an absolute constant.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, if there exists some <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},d_{\text{intrinsic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},d_{\text{intrinsic}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\delta}(K)\leq C_{1}\delta^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\delta}(K)\leq C_{1}\delta^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all positive <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists some <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=C_{2}(C_{1},n,d_{\text{intrinsic}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=C_{2}(C_{1},n,d_{\text{intrinsic}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that any function <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy by a ReLU network whose total number of parameters is bounded by <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The rate of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained in the corollary is an improvement by a factor of two (inside the exponent) over the <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> factor obtained in previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakada and Imaizumi (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The purpose of this section is to discuss approximation rates for functions which are invariant to both permutations and rigid motions. We will divide our discussion into two subsections. The first subsection will discuss the approximation rates of (non-invariant) ReLU neural networks. The second subsection will utilize these results, together with our approximation rates for Deep Sets, to prove approximation rates for invariant models which are constructed by applying a rotation frame to a Deep Sets permutation invariant backbone.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>ReLU approximation rates</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal in this subsection is to determine approximation rates for (non-invariant) ReLU networks. Our first and most technically involved result will pertain to functions which are invariant only to rotations:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(ReLU approximation rates for <math id="Thmtheorem6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers, and let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the compact set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq 1,\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq 1,\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exist <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W(d,\alpha),L=L(d,\alpha),q=q(d,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W(d,\alpha),L=L(d,\alpha),q=q(d,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for all <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant function <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, can be approximated to <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on the set <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by a neural network with width <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(O(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(O(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a corollary for this theorem, we can easily get approximation rates for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[ReLU approximation rates for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions]
Let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers and let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/2}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/2}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the compact set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{1/2}=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq\frac{1}{2},\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/2}=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq\frac{1}{2},\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then there exist <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W(d,\alpha),L=L(d,\alpha),q=q(d,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W(d,\alpha),L=L(d,\alpha),q=q(d,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for all <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant function <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on the set <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> by a neural network with width <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any point cloud <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> we define <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to be the mapping which translates all coordinates of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by the last coordinate, namely</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X)=(x_{1}-x_{n},\ldots,x_{n-1}-x_{n},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X)=(x_{1}-x_{n},\ldots,x_{n-1}-x_{n},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The image of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{K}=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})|\|x_{j}\|\leq 1,x_{n}=0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})|\|x_{j}\|\leq 1,x_{n}=0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">which can be identified with the <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional set <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from the theorem, by ignoring the last coordinate which is always zero. By the theorem, on <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> we can approximate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy using a ReLU network <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> with width <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{(n-1)d-\dim(O(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{(n-1)d-\dim(O(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the approximation rate advertised in the corollary, since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)d-\dim(O(d))=nd-\dim(E(d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)d-\dim(O(d))=nd-\dim(E(d))</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for any <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in K_{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in K_{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|f(X)-\psi(TX)\|=|f(TX)-\psi(TX)\|\leq\epsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(X)-\psi(TX)\|=|f(TX)-\psi(TX)\|\leq\epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">and since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(TX)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(TX)</annotation></semantics></math> is a composition of the neural network <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> with the linear function <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, it too is a ReLU network of the dimensions advertised in the corollary.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we can achieve a similar result for functions invariant not only to rigid motions, but also to permutations. Since the permutation group is finite, the approximation rates we would expect in this case are identical, and since in this setting the functions we are interested in are invariant to rigid motions, the approximation rates we have already obtained still apply. In other words we obtain another trivial corollary:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">The approximation rates for approximating <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions by ReLU networks, stated in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (ReLU approximation rates for 𝑂(𝑑) invariant functions). ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, are also applicable for <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions. The rates for approximating <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions by ReLU networks, stated in Corollary <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, are also applicable for <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions.

The rest of this section is devoted to proving Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (ReLU approximation rates for 𝑂(𝑑) invariant functions). ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. At very high level, the idea of the proof is that, one can choose <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points from the point cloud, e.g., <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and, assuming they are linearly independent, one can use the Gram Schmidt procedure to turn them into an orthonormal basis <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same span, for all <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to be the orthonormal matrix whose rows are <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)=f(UX)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)=f(UX)</annotation></semantics></math>, and that since <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x_{i},u_{j}\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x_{i},u_{j}\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, the point cloud <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UX</annotation></semantics></math> will reside in a linear subspace</p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad x_{i}(j)=0,\forall 1\leq i&lt;j\leq d\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad x_{i}(j)=0,\forall 1\leq i&lt;j\leq d\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">whose dimension is exactly the dimension of the quotient space, namely <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\cdot d-\frac{d^{2}-d}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\cdot d-\frac{d^{2}-d}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. At a high level, this reduces the problem to the problem of approximation on this subspace <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and on this subspace we can get the approximation rates we want since its dimension is the dimension of the quotient space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The challenges in implementing this strategy are that we do not always have that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent. In some cases, there could be a different <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple which is linearly independent. In other, it may be that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not of rank <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. We will handle these challenges in a way similar to the frame constructions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A unique orthogonal basis for <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be determined in an <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant way, can actually be obtained using only <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> vectors and their (generalized) vector product. We choose to use <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> vectors to define the basis since it simplifies the analysis.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Preliminaries I: Gram Schmidt Stability</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As preliminaries for the proof, we will introduce two lemmas regarding the stability of the Gram-Schmidt procedure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Gram-Schmidt procedure is based on repeated application of the two functions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S4.E69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=x,\quad p(x;u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})=x-\sum_{i=1}^{k}\langle x,u_{i}\rangle u_{i},\quad N(y)=\frac{y}{\|y\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=x,\quad p(x;u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})=x-\sum_{i=1}^{k}\langle x,u_{i}\rangle u_{i},\quad N(y)=\frac{y}{\|y\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(69)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In terms of these function, the Gram Schmidt procedure for given <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq d</annotation></semantics></math> linearly independent vectors <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined recursively via</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p(x_{j};u_{1},\ldots,u_{j-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p(x_{j};u_{1},\ldots,u_{j-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=N(v_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=N(v_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with two lemmas on the stability of the functions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to perturbations of their input:
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
For <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orthonormal family, and let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|u_{j}-\tilde{u}_{j}\|\leq\eta,\forall j=1,\ldots,k" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u_{j}-\tilde{u}_{j}\|\leq\eta,\forall j=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Denote</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p(x|u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p(x|u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p(x|\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p(x|\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of norm <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v-\tilde{v}\|\leq 3k\eta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v-\tilde{v}\|\leq 3k\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using the triangle and Cauchy-Schwarz inequalities, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|v-\tilde{v}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|v-\tilde{v}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\|\langle x,u_{i}\rangle u_{i}-\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle\tilde{u}_{i}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\|\langle x,u_{i}\rangle u_{i}-\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle\tilde{u}_{i}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(\|\langle x,u_{i}\rangle u_{i}-\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle u_{i}\|+\|\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle u_{i}-\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle\tilde{u}_{i}\|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(\|\langle x,u_{i}\rangle u_{i}-\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle u_{i}\|+\|\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle u_{i}-\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle\tilde{u}_{i}\|\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(|\langle x,u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle|\|u_{i}\|+|\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle|\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(|\langle x,u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle|\|u_{i}\|+|\langle x,\tilde{u}_{i}\rangle|\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(\|x\|\|u_{i}\|\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|+\|x\|\|\tilde{u}_{i}\|\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(\|x\|\|u_{i}\|\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|+\|x\|\|\tilde{u}_{i}\|\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 3k\eta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 3k\eta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where for the last inequality we used the fact that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{u}_{i}\|\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{u}_{i}\|\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> since</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{u}_{i}\|\leq\|u_{i}\|+\|\tilde{u}_{i}-u_{i}\|\leq 1+\eta\leq 2" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{u}_{i}\|\leq\|u_{i}\|+\|\tilde{u}_{i}-u_{i}\|\leq 1+\eta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z,\tilde{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z,\tilde{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|z\|\geq\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|z\|\geq\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|z-\tilde{z}\|\leq\beta&lt;\alpha/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|z-\tilde{z}\|\leq\beta&lt;\alpha/2</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|N(z)-N(\tilde{z})\|\leq\frac{4\beta}{\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|N(z)-N(\tilde{z})\|\leq\frac{4\beta}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|N(z)-N(\tilde{z})\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|N(z)-N(\tilde{z})\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\|z\|\cdot\|\tilde{z}\|}\cdot\|\|\tilde{z}\|\cdot z-\|z\|\cdot\tilde{z}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\|z\|\cdot\|\tilde{z}\|}\cdot\|\|\tilde{z}\|\cdot z-\|z\|\cdot\tilde{z}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{\|z\|\cdot\|\tilde{z}\|}\cdot\left(\|\|\tilde{z}\|\cdot z-\|z\|\cdot z\|+\|\|z\|\cdot z-\|z\|\cdot\tilde{z}\|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{\|z\|\cdot\|\tilde{z}\|}\cdot\left(\|\|\tilde{z}\|\cdot z-\|z\|\cdot z\|+\|\|z\|\cdot z-\|z\|\cdot\tilde{z}\|\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{\|z\|\cdot\|\tilde{z}\|}\cdot 2\|z\|\cdot\|z-\tilde{z}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{\|z\|\cdot\|\tilde{z}\|}\cdot 2\|z\|\cdot\|z-\tilde{z}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{4\beta}{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{4\beta}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where we use the fact that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{z}\|\geq\|z\|-\|\tilde{z}-z\|\geq\alpha-\beta\geq\frac{\alpha}{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{z}\|\geq\|z\|-\|\tilde{z}-z\|\geq\alpha-\beta\geq\frac{\alpha}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Preliminaries II: Super approximation and the root function</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Important for our proof is the fact that the Gram-Schmidt procedure is composed of arithmetic operations which can be ‘super-approximated’ by ReLU networks. The function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial, composed of a finite number of multiplication and addition operations. Addition can be implemented exactly by neural networks. For multiplication, Proposition 3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2017)</a></cite> states that the function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\mapsto x\cdot y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\mapsto x\cdot y</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-M,M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-M,M]</annotation></semantics></math> by a ReLU network with fixed width and depth proportional to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1/\epsilon)+\log(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1/\epsilon)+\log(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this, it can be shown that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy with only <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\log(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\log(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(z)=\frac{z}{\|z\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(z)=\frac{z}{\|z\|}</annotation></semantics></math> is the composition of the function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(z,s)=z/s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(z,s)=z/s</annotation></semantics></math> with the function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|z\|=\sqrt{\|z\|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|z\|=\sqrt{\|z\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|z\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|z\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial and hence we have super approximation for this function. Similarly, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Telgarsky (2017)</a></cite>, Theorem 1.1,
the rational function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]^{d}\times[\eta,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]^{d}\times[\eta,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, by a neural network with fixed width and depth which is bounded by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln^{7}(1/\eta)\ln^{3}(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln^{7}(1/\eta)\ln^{3}(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. The only remaining gap is similar super approximation results for the root function, which to the best of our knowledge have not been proven so far. We address this gap with the following lemma, which follows using similar techniques to the argument in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Telgarsky (2017)</a></cite>.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon\leq 1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon\leq 1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a deep ReLU neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\in\Upsilon^{21,L}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\in\Upsilon^{21,L}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq C|\log(\epsilon)|^{3}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq C|\log(\epsilon)|^{3}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<table id="S4.E70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x\in[0,1]}|\mathcal{N}(x)-x^{\alpha}|&lt;C\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x\in[0,1]}|\mathcal{N}(x)-x^{\alpha}|&lt;C\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(70)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that the logarithms here are taken base <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and the constants depend only upon <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by showing that there is a network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{0}\in\Upsilon^{17,L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{0}\in\Upsilon^{17,L}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq C|\log(\epsilon)^{2}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq C|\log(\epsilon)^{2}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|</annotation></semantics></math> such that (here the constant <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> depends upon <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></p>
<table id="S4.E71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x\in[1/2,1]}|\mathcal{N}_{0}(x)-x^{\alpha}|&lt;\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x\in[1/2,1]}|\mathcal{N}_{0}(x)-x^{\alpha}|&lt;\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(71)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">To do this, we utilize the Taylor expansion of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> about <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\alpha}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}(x-1)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\alpha}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}(x-1)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(72)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Using Taylor’s estimate for the remainder, and the fact that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}\right|\leq C_{\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}\right|\leq C_{\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(73)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">uniformly in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the following bound for the error incurred by truncating this series after <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> terms:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x\in[1/2,1]}\left|x^{\alpha}-\sum_{n=0}^{k}\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}(x-1)^{n}\right|\leq C_{\alpha}2^{-k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x\in[1/2,1]}\left|x^{\alpha}-\sum_{n=0}^{k}\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}(x-1)^{n}\right|\leq C_{\alpha}2^{-k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(74)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, the coefficients in this expansion are also bounded by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x-1|\leq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x-1|\leq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use the construction of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2017)</a></cite> (see for instance also Proposition 3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite>) and repeated squaring, to approximate the term</span></p>
<table id="S4.E75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}(x-1)^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}(x-1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(75)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">to error <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> using a neural network in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon^{15,L}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon^{15,L}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq C\log(n)|\log(\delta)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq C\log(n)|\log(\delta)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now choose <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> large enough so that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\alpha}2^{-k}&lt;\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\alpha}2^{-k}&lt;\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. This requires <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=O(|\log(\epsilon)|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=O(|\log(\epsilon)|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we apply this result for each term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,...,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,...,k</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{n}=\epsilon 2^{-{n+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{n}=\epsilon 2^{-{n+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Summing up these networks, we obtain the desired network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{N}_{0}(x)-x^{\alpha}|&lt;\epsilon/2+\epsilon\sum_{n=0}^{k}2^{-n+1}\leq\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{N}_{0}(x)-x^{\alpha}|&lt;\epsilon/2+\epsilon\sum_{n=0}^{k}2^{-n+1}\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(76)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Counting the size of the network, we see that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{0}\in\Upsilon^{17,L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{0}\in\Upsilon^{17,L}</annotation></semantics></math> (the summing operation requires the width to be increased by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>) with</span></p>
<table id="S4.E77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}L&amp;\leq C\sum_{n=0}^{k}\log(n)|\log(\epsilon 2^{-{n+1}})|\\
&amp;\leq C\sum_{n=0}^{k}\log(n)(|\log(\epsilon)|+(n+1))\\
&amp;\leq C(k^{2}\log(k)+k\log(k)|\log(\epsilon)|)\leq C|\log(\epsilon)^{2}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>L</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}L&amp;\leq C\sum_{n=0}^{k}\log(n)|\log(\epsilon 2^{-{n+1}})|\\
&amp;\leq C\sum_{n=0}^{k}\log(n)(|\log(\epsilon)|+(n+1))\\
&amp;\leq C(k^{2}\log(k)+k\log(k)|\log(\epsilon)|)\leq C|\log(\epsilon)^{2}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(77)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Next, for each integer <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a piecewise linear function</span></p>
<table id="S4.E78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{j}(x)=\begin{cases}1&amp;2^{-j/3-1}\leq x\leq 2^{-j/3}\\
0&amp;x&lt;2^{-j/3-1.1}\\
0&amp;x&gt;2^{-j/3+0.1}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{j}(x)=\begin{cases}1&amp;2^{-j/3-1}\leq x\leq 2^{-j/3}\\
0&amp;x&lt;2^{-j/3-1.1}\\
0&amp;x&gt;2^{-j/3+0.1}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(78)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">We clearly have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{j}\in\Upsilon^{4,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{j}\in\Upsilon^{4,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now construct the functions</span></p>
<table id="S4.E79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{k}2^{-\alpha n}\phi_{3n+t}(x)\mathcal{N}_{0}(2^{n}x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{k}2^{-\alpha n}\phi_{3n+t}(x)\mathcal{N}_{0}(2^{n}x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(79)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> have the following property:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.E80.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">for all <math id="S4.E80.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> there are at least two indices <math id="S4.E80.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.E80.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{3n+t}(x)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{3n+t}(x)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.E80.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(80)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Utilizing the product and sum network constructions again we see that each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated to accuracy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with a network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}\in\Upsilon^{19,L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}\in\Upsilon^{19,L}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq Ck|\log(\epsilon)\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq Ck|\log(\epsilon)\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we remark that the product network construction is exact if one term is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2017)</a></cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite> for example). Hence the network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on the interval <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,2^{k/3-1.1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,2^{k/3-1.1}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choosing <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{k\alpha/3}\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{k\alpha/3}\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, from which it follows that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=O(|\log(\epsilon)|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=O(|\log(\epsilon)|)</annotation></semantics></math> (for a constant depending upon <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>), the networks <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> will have depth <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq C|\log(\epsilon)^{3}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq C|\log(\epsilon)^{3}\log(|\log(\epsilon)|)|</annotation></semantics></math>, and moreover will satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.E81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{for all $x\in[0,1]$},~~|\mathcal{N}_{t}(x)-x|\leq C\epsilon,~\text{for at least two indices $t$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>for at least two indices </mtext><mi>t</mi><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{for all $x\in[0,1]$},~~|\mathcal{N}_{t}(x)-x|\leq C\epsilon,~\text{for at least two indices $t$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(81)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;2^{k/3-1.1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;2^{k/3-1.1}</annotation></semantics></math> this follows since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x^{\alpha}|=|x|^{\alpha}\leq 2^{k\alpha/3}\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x^{\alpha}|=|x|^{\alpha}\leq 2^{k\alpha/3}\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (by our choice of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, while for the other values of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> it follows from the approximation result for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the homogeneity of the function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, the property (<a href="#S4.E80" title="Equation 80 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.1.2 Preliminaries II: Super approximation and the root function ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">80</span></a>), and the fact that the product network construction is exact if one term is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence only a fixed number of terms in (<a href="#S4.E79" title="Equation 79 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.1.2 Preliminaries II: Super approximation and the root function ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>) incur a non-zero error).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, taking the median of the three networks <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{0},\mathcal{N}_{1},\mathcal{N}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{0},\mathcal{N}_{1},\mathcal{N}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the result (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel (2023)</a></cite>, Corollary 13).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now that we have efficient approximation of root functions, we can prove that the functions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> used to construct the Gram-Schmidt procedure can be approximated efficiently, as long as the input to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded away from zero:
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,k</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,\delta,\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,\delta,\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> be positive numbers. Then there exist constants <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=C_{1}(d,k,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=C_{1}(d,k,M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=C_{2}(d,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=C_{2}(d,M)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, defined in (<a href="#S4.E69" title="Equation 69 ‣ 4.1.1 Preliminaries I: Gram Schmidt Stability ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>), can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-M,M]^{d\times(k+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-M,M]^{d\times(k+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> by a ReLU network with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq C_{1}\left(\log(|\epsilon|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq C_{1}\left(\log(|\epsilon|\right)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, also defined in (<a href="#S4.E69" title="Equation 69 ‣ 4.1.1 Preliminaries I: Gram Schmidt Stability ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>), can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\delta,M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\delta,M]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq C\left(\log^{3}(|\epsilon|\cdot\log^{7}(|\delta|)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq C\left(\log^{3}(|\epsilon|\cdot\log^{7}(|\delta|)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">As mentioned above, the approximation result for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a polynomial, appears in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2017)</a></cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as a composition</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(x)=q(x,r\circ s(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(x)=q(x,r\circ s(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x,t)=x/t,\quad r(t)=\sqrt{t}\text{ and }s(x)=\|x\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x,t)=x/t,\quad r(t)=\sqrt{t}\text{ and }s(x)=\|x\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">Combining the approximation results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky (2017)</a></cite> for the polynomial <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> with our approximation results for the square root and the approximation results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Telgarsky (2017)</a></cite>, Theorem 1.1,
for the rational function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> gives us the required result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (ReLU approximation rates for 𝑂(𝑑) invariant functions). ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For given <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we define the set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{1}(\delta)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}|\quad\delta\leq\|x\|\leq 1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{1}(\delta)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}|\quad\delta\leq\|x\|\leq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq d</annotation></semantics></math> we define recursively</p>
<table id="S4.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k}(\delta)=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}|(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in\mathcal{D}_{k-1}(\delta)\quad\text{ and }\|x_{k}\|\leq 1\text{ and }\|p(x_{k};u_{1},\ldots,u_{k-1})\|\geq\delta\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≥</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k}(\delta)=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}|(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in\mathcal{D}_{k-1}(\delta)\quad\text{ and }\|x_{k}\|\leq 1\text{ and }\|p(x_{k};u_{1},\ldots,u_{k-1})\|\geq\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}=u_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}=u_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th vector obtained from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> during the Gram-Schmidt procedure. We prove
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
Fix some natural <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
There exist <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W(d),L=L(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W(d),L=L(d)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that,
for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist relu neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, with width <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L\log(1/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L\log(1/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{u}_{i}(X)-u_{i}(X)\|\leq\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{d+1-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{u}_{i}(X)-u_{i}(X)\|\leq\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{d+1-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Denote</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{k}=\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{d+1-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{k}=\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{d+1-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By induction on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the cardinality of the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x_{i}\|\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x_{i}\|\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we can build a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{N}</annotation></semantics></math> approximating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to accuracy of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a number of parameters which depends poly-logarithmically on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (see Lemma <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="4.1.2 Preliminaries II: Super approximation and the root function ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now assume correctness for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let us prove the claim for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 Preliminaries I: Gram Schmidt Stability ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a>, we know that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|p(x_{i_{k+1}};u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})-p(x_{i_{k+1}};\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k})\|\leq 3k\cdot\eta_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|p(x_{i_{k+1}};u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})-p(x_{i_{k+1}};\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k})\|\leq 3k\cdot\eta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">While <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is not a neural network, it is a polynomial, and so we can obtain an approximation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of it of order <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\eta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\eta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}_{k+1}=\tilde{p}(x_{i_{k+1}};\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{k+1}=\tilde{p}(x_{i_{k+1}};\tilde{u}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">is a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4k\eta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4k\eta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}=p(x_{i_{k+1}};u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}=p(x_{i_{k+1}};u_{1},\ldots,u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">and the number of parameters in the neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}</annotation></semantics></math> grows like <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log|\eta_{k}|\sim\log|\delta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log|\eta_{k}|\sim\log|\delta|</annotation></semantics></math>
By assumption, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|z_{k+1}\|\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|z_{k+1}\|\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and so applying Lemma <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 Preliminaries I: Gram Schmidt Stability ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=4k\eta_{k}\leq 4k\frac{\delta}{20d}\leq\frac{4}{20}\cdot\delta\leq\frac{\alpha}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=4k\eta_{k}\leq 4k\frac{\delta}{20d}\leq\frac{4}{20}\cdot\delta\leq\frac{\alpha}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">we obtain that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|N(z_{k+1})-N(\tilde{z}_{k+1})\|\leq 16k\frac{\eta_{k}}{\delta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|N(z_{k+1})-N(\tilde{z}_{k+1})\|\leq 16k\frac{\eta_{k}}{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">While <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is not a neural network, we can replace it with a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed width, and depth depending logarithmically on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{N}(\tilde{z}_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{N}(\tilde{z}_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k\eta_{k})/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k\eta_{k})/\delta</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(\tilde{z}_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(\tilde{z}_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. All in all, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{k+1}=\tilde{N}(\tilde{z}_{k+1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{k+1}=\tilde{N}(\tilde{z}_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.7.1" class="ltx_text">as an output of a neural network whose size depends logarithmically on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|u_{k+1}-\tilde{u}_{k+1}\|\leq\frac{17k}{\delta}\eta_{k}\leq\frac{20d}{\delta}\eta_{k}=\eta_{k+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u_{k+1}-\tilde{u}_{k+1}\|\leq\frac{17k}{\delta}\eta_{k}\leq\frac{20d}{\delta}\eta_{k}=\eta_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.8.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, for a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}=(i_{1},\ldots,i_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}=(i_{1},\ldots,i_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S4.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)=\{X\in K|\quad(x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{k}})\in\mathcal{D}_{k}(\delta)\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)=\{X\in K|\quad(x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{k}})\in\mathcal{D}_{k}(\delta)\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We will also allow a definition of a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will define</p>
<table id="S4.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\emptyset,\delta)=K." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\emptyset,\delta)=K.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">We will now conclude by proving the following lemma:
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the compact set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq 1,\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq 1,\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Then there exist <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W(d),L=L(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W(d),L=L(d)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq k\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq k\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on the set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S4.E82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq\min\left\{1,\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\epsilon}{4(\sqrt{nd}+1)^{\alpha}}\right)^{1/\alpha}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq\min\left\{1,\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\epsilon}{4(\sqrt{nd}+1)^{\alpha}}\right)^{1/\alpha}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(82)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.4" class="ltx_p">by a neural network with width <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(O(d))}{2\alpha}}\log(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(O(d))}{2\alpha}}\log(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>

We note that Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (ReLU approximation rates for 𝑂(𝑑) invariant functions). ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> follows from the lemma in the special case where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> tuple <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove by induction on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the cardinality of the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tuple <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base case: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We denote by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> the vectors obtained from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> via Gram-Schmidt. Throughout we supress the dependence of these vectors on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for readability. By Lemma <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS3" title="4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.3</span></a>, we know that, for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,d</annotation></semantics></math> there is a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}=\tilde{u}_{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}=\tilde{u}_{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></span></p>
<table id="S4.E83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|\leq\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|\leq\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(83)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be the orthonormal matrix whose rows are the vectors <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix whose rows are <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UX</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to the vector space</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(\mathbf{i})=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad x_{i_{j}}(s)=0,\forall 1\leq j&lt;s\leq d\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathbf{i})=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad x_{i_{j}}(s)=0,\forall 1\leq j&lt;s\leq d\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">whose dimension is exactly the dimension of the quotient space, namely <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\cdot d-\frac{d^{2}-d}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\cdot d-\frac{d^{2}-d}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Corollary <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we can approximate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on the intersection of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the compact set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}=\{X|\|X\|_{F}\leq 2\sqrt{nd}+1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}=\{X|\|X\|_{F}\leq 2\sqrt{nd}+1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.4.1" class="ltx_text">to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy, with a ReLU network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{n\cdot d-\frac{d^{2}-d}{2}}{2\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{n\cdot d-\frac{d^{2}-d}{2}}{2\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>
parameters. Note that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly contained in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> as the norm of each column of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in K</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is “almost a rotation”, in the sense that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|U-\tilde{U}\|_{F}^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|^{2}\leq d\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{2}\leq\delta^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|U-\tilde{U}\|_{F}^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\|u_{i}-\tilde{u}_{i}\|^{2}\leq d\left(\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}\right)^{2}\leq\delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(84)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">We can approximate the product <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{U}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{U}X</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy, by a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\tilde{U},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\tilde{U},X)</annotation></semantics></math> whose size depends logaritmically on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex81"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|UX-M(\tilde{U},X)\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|UX-M(\tilde{U},X)\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex81.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\|(U-\tilde{U})X\|_{F}+\|\tilde{U}X-M(\tilde{U},X)\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\|(U-\tilde{U})X\|_{F}+\|\tilde{U}X-M(\tilde{U},X)\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex82"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\|U-\tilde{U}\|_{F}\|X\|_{F}+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\|U-\tilde{U}\|_{F}\|X\|_{F}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex83"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\delta(\sqrt{nd}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\delta(\sqrt{nd}+1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our approximation for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on the domain will be <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection onto the space <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbf{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbf{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex84"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(X)-\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(X)-\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex84.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=|f\circ\pi(UX))-\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|f\circ\pi(UX))-\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex85"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq|f\circ\pi(UX))-f\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq|f\circ\pi(UX))-f\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex86"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+|f\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)-\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+|f\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)-\tilde{f}\circ\pi\circ M\left(\tilde{U}(X),X\right)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex87"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\stackrel{{\scriptstyle(*)}}{{\leq}}\|UX-M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}^{\alpha}+\frac{\epsilon}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\stackrel{{\scriptstyle(*)}}{{\leq}}\|UX-M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}^{\alpha}+\frac{\epsilon}{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex88"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\stackrel{{\scriptstyle(**)}}{{\leq}}\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\stackrel{{\scriptstyle(**)}}{{\leq}}\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">where for (*) we used the fact that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is projection, as well as the fact that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbf{i}}\cap\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbf{i}}\cap\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\tilde{U}(X),X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\tilde{U}(X),X)</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, since</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}\leq\|UX\|_{F}+\|UX-M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}\leq\|X\|_{F}+\delta(\sqrt{nd}+1)\leq 2\sqrt{nd}+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}\leq\|UX\|_{F}+\|UX-M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}\leq\|X\|_{F}+\delta(\sqrt{nd}+1)\leq 2\sqrt{nd}+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">For (**) we used (<a href="#S4.E82" title="Equation 82 ‣ 4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Induction step<span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Now assume that the claim is true for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> tuples, we want to prove correctness for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tuples. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple, and for an index <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> tuple obtained by concatenating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}\circ j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐢</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}\circ j</annotation></semantics></math>. To construct an approximation for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbf{i}}\cap K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbf{i}}\cap K</annotation></semantics></math>, we note that for every index <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of the desired size, giving an <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on the intersection of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex90" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i}\circ j,\delta)=\{X\in V_{\mathbf{i}},\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|\geq\delta\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐢</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i}\circ j,\delta)=\{X\in V_{\mathbf{i}},\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|\geq\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">The set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is covered by the union of all these sets, with the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex91" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)=\{X\in V_{\mathbf{i}}|\quad\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|\leq 2\delta,\quad\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)=\{X\in V_{\mathbf{i}}|\quad\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|\leq 2\delta,\quad\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.3.1" class="ltx_text">On the set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can approximate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with a neural network to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy, with the desired approximation rate, as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we use Lemma <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS3" title="4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.3</span></a> to construct neural networks <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i_{1}}(X),\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i_{1}}(X),\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> with a number of parameters logarithmic in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, such that (<a href="#S4.E83" title="Equation 83 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">83</span></a>) for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. We then denote by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U,\tilde{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U,\tilde{U}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex92" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\begin{pmatrix}-u_{i_{1}}-\\
\vdots\\
-u_{i_{k}}-\\
-0_{d}-\\
\vdots\\
-0_{d}-\end{pmatrix},\quad\tilde{U}=\begin{pmatrix}-\tilde{u}_{i_{1}}-\\
\vdots\\
-\tilde{u}_{i_{k}}-\\
-0_{d}-\\
\vdots\\
-0_{d}-\end{pmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\begin{pmatrix}-u_{i_{1}}-\\
\vdots\\
-u_{i_{k}}-\\
-0_{d}-\\
\vdots\\
-0_{d}-\end{pmatrix},\quad\tilde{U}=\begin{pmatrix}-\tilde{u}_{i_{1}}-\\
\vdots\\
-\tilde{u}_{i_{k}}-\\
-0_{d}-\\
\vdots\\
-0_{d}-\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">and we have that (<a href="#S4.E84" title="Equation 84 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">84</span></a>) holds.
and we denote by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> the orthonormal matrix obtained by replacing the zero rows of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> by orthonormal rows. We then have that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)=f(\hat{U}X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)=f(\hat{U}X)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex93"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(X)-f(UX)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(X)-f(UX)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex93.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|f(\hat{U}X)-f(UX)|\leq\|\hat{U}X-UX\|_{F}^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|f(\hat{U}X)-f(UX)|\leq\|\hat{U}X-UX\|_{F}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex94"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|(\hat{U}-U)x_{j}\|_{2}^{2}\right)^{\alpha/2}\leq\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|^{2}\right)^{\alpha/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|(\hat{U}-U)x_{j}\|_{2}^{2}\right)^{\alpha/2}\leq\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|^{2}\right)^{\alpha/2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex95"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq(n(2\delta)^{2})^{\alpha/2}=(2\delta)^{\alpha}n^{\alpha/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq(n(2\delta)^{2})^{\alpha/2}=(2\delta)^{\alpha}n^{\alpha/2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">By the definition of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E82" title="Equation 82 ‣ 4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>), since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{n}\leq\sqrt{nd}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{n}\leq\sqrt{nd}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\delta)^{\alpha}n^{\alpha/2}\leq\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\delta)^{\alpha}n^{\alpha/2}\leq\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(UX)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(UX)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, we note that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UX</annotation></semantics></math> is in the vector space</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|x_{i_{j}}(s)=0,\forall 1\leq j&lt;s\leq k\text{ and }x_{t}(s)=0,\forall t=1,\ldots,n,\quad s=k+1,\ldots,d\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|x_{i_{j}}(s)=0,\forall 1\leq j&lt;s\leq k\text{ and }x_{t}(s)=0,\forall t=1,\ldots,n,\quad s=k+1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.4.1" class="ltx_text">We note that for any completion of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-index with distinct elements <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})\subseteq\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐣</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})\subseteq\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})\cap\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})\cap\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> by a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy with the approximation rate we desire, using Corollary <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. We denote the projection onto <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{i})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and we then obtain for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex97"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(X)-\tilde{f}_{0}\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(X)-\tilde{f}_{0}\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex97.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq|f(X)-f(UX)|+|f\circ\pi(UX)-\tilde{f}_{0}\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq|f(X)-f(UX)|+|f\circ\pi(UX)-\tilde{f}_{0}\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex98"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon/4+|f\circ\pi(UX)-f\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon/4+|f\circ\pi(UX)-f\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex99"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+|f\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)-\tilde{f}_{0}\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+|f\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)-\tilde{f}_{0}\circ\pi\circ M(\tilde{U}(X),X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex100"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon/4+\|UX-M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}^{\alpha}+\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon/4+\|UX-M(\tilde{U}(X),X)\|_{F}^{\alpha}+\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex101"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon/4+\delta^{\alpha}(\sqrt{nd}+1)^{\alpha}+\epsilon/4\leq 3\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon/4+\delta^{\alpha}(\sqrt{nd}+1)^{\alpha}+\epsilon/4\leq 3\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality uses (<a href="#S4.E82" title="Equation 82 ‣ 4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>), which gives <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\alpha}(\sqrt{nd}+1)^{\alpha}\leq\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\alpha}(\sqrt{nd}+1)^{\alpha}\leq\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math>. All in all we obtain a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> approximation for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p15.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now that we have an approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by ReLU networks on a collection of sets which cover the domain <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we will combine these ReLU networks to a global approximation using a partition of unity argument. For <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> we define the piecewise linear function</span></p>
<table id="S4.E85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{a,b}(x)=\begin{cases}0&amp;x\leq a\\
\frac{x-a}{b-a}&amp;x\in[a,b]\\
1&amp;x\geq b.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{a,b}(x)=\begin{cases}0&amp;x\leq a\\
\frac{x-a}{b-a}&amp;x\in[a,b]\\
1&amp;x\geq b.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(85)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">This can be realized as a ReLU network of depth 1 and width 2. Another ingredient we will need for the partition of unity is a ReLU network approximation of the function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Lemma <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS3" title="4.1.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.3</span></a> we approximate the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with an error of at most <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 Preliminaries I: Gram Schmidt Stability ‣ 4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a> we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\|p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|-\|p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\||\leq\|p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})-p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\|\leq 3d\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}=\frac{3\delta}{20}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\|p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|-\|p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\||\leq\|p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})-p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\|\leq 3d\frac{\delta}{20\cdot d}=\frac{3\delta}{20}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|p\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|p\|</annotation></semantics></math> is a composition of a polynomial with the root function, we can approximate it to accuracy of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/20</annotation></semantics></math>, with a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\log(1/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\log(1/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters, and we will have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex103"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|-\nu(x_{j};\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\|p(x_{j};u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|-\nu(x_{j};\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex103.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq|\|p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|-\|p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\||" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq|\|p(x;u_{i_{1}},\ldots,u_{i_{k}})\|-\|p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\||</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex104"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad+|\|p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\|-\nu(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad+|\|p(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})\|-\nu(x;\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex105"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{\delta}{20}+\frac{3\delta}{20}=\frac{\delta}{5}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{\delta}{20}+\frac{3\delta}{20}=\frac{\delta}{5}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us now relabel <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(x_{j};\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(x_{j};\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex106" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex106.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{j}(X):=\nu(x_{j};\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{j}(X):=\nu(x_{j};\tilde{u}_{i_{1}},\ldots,\tilde{u}_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will approximate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by the function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex107"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex107.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex107.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min_{1\leq j\leq n}\phi_{a,b}\left(\nu_{j}(X)\right),\text{ where }a=\frac{8\delta}{5},b=\frac{9\delta}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min_{1\leq j\leq n}\phi_{a,b}\left(\nu_{j}(X)\right),\text{ where }a=\frac{8\delta}{5},b=\frac{9\delta}{5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex108"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex108.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex108.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\phi_{a,b}\left(\nu_{j}(X)\right),\text{ where }a=\frac{6\delta}{5},b=\frac{7\delta}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\phi_{a,b}\left(\nu_{j}(X)\right),\text{ where }a=\frac{6\delta}{5},b=\frac{7\delta}{5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex109"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex109.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex109.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\phi_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\phi_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex110"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex110.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{f}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{f}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex110.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=(1-\psi(X))\tilde{f}_{0}(X)+\psi(X)\cdot\frac{1}{N(X)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\phi_{j}(X)\tilde{f}_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1-\psi(X))\tilde{f}_{0}(X)+\psi(X)\cdot\frac{1}{N(X)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\phi_{j}(X)\tilde{f}_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">We note that the coefficient <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\psi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\psi(X)</annotation></semantics></math> multiplying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is not zero, only when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{j}(X)&lt;\frac{9\delta}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{j}(X)&lt;\frac{9\delta}{5}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that all projections of the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate have norm of at most <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and so for all such <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the value <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is not zero, we can deduce that there exists some <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{j}(X)&gt;\frac{8\delta}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{j}(X)&gt;\frac{8\delta}{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus the norm of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{j};x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{j};x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{7\delta}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{7\delta}{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for this <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> we will have that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{j}(X)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{j}(X)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this means that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(X)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(X)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and so the division by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined. In fact, for any <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{j}(X)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{j}(X)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{j}(X)&gt;\frac{6\delta}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{j}(X)&gt;\frac{6\delta}{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus the norm of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{j};x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{j};x_{i_{1}},\ldots,x_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{i},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that when the coefficient <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(X)\cdot\phi_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(X)\cdot\phi_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is not zero, we know that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (by the inductive hypothesis). It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex111"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex111.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(X)-\hat{f}(X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(X)-\hat{f}(X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex111.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|(1-\psi(X))(f(X)-\tilde{f}_{0}(X))+\psi(X)\frac{1}{N(X)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\phi_{j}(X)(\tilde{f}_{j}(X)-f(X))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|(1-\psi(X))(f(X)-\tilde{f}_{0}(X))+\psi(X)\frac{1}{N(X)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\phi_{j}(X)(\tilde{f}_{j}(X)-f(X))|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex112"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq(1-\psi(X))\cdot 3\epsilon/4+\psi(X)\cdot\epsilon\leq\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq(1-\psi(X))\cdot 3\epsilon/4+\psi(X)\cdot\epsilon\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we note that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by product and summations or neural networks, and this can be <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math> approximated using neural networks which approximate multiplications, with an additional cost of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\log(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\log(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. This concludes the proof of the lemma, and the proof of the theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Approximation rates for rotation invariant function spaces using frames</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frames are a popular recent notion in the design of invariant models for point sets and other datatypes. For a group <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acting on a collection of point sets, we define a frame to be any mapping <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto\mu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto\mu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> which assigns to each point set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a measure <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This is used to define a generalized averaging operation on functions <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> defined on point sets is, via</p>
<table id="S4.E86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[f](X)=\int_{G}f(g^{-1}X)d\mu_{X}(g)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[f](X)=\int_{G}f(g^{-1}X)d\mu_{X}(g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(86)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To ensure that the function <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[f]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[f]</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant, one can require that the mapping <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto\mu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto\mu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant, or weakly <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> equivariant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2024)</a></cite> (though our approximation results will not require these equivariance assumptions). One simple example of a frame is the mapping which takes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to the Haar measure on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case the frames performs a standard group averaging procedure. Another family of examples are canonicalization algorithms, where each <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on a single element, see e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From a practical perspective, an advantage of frame based models is they can apply a successful non-invariant model <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to the data, and enforce invariance via the generalized averaging operation to obtain an invariant model <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[f]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[f]</annotation></semantics></math>. From the approximation perspective, we have a similar advantage here. Namely, approximation rate we have for a ReLU neural network <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, will be inherited by frame-averaged <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[f]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[f]</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular we can adapt Corollary <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> to obtain</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{prop}</span>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[Frame approximation rates for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant functions]
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq d+1</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers and let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=E(d)\times S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=E(d)\times S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> be a frame averaging operator as in (<a href="#S4.E86" title="Equation 86 ‣ 4.2 Approximation rates for rotation invariant function spaces using frames ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">86</span></a>). Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/4}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/4}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the compact set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex113" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex113.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{1/4}=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq\frac{1}{4},\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/4}=\{(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\quad\|x_{j}\|\leq\frac{1}{4},\forall j=1,\ldots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then there exist <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W(d,\alpha),L=L(d,\alpha),q=q(d,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W(d,\alpha),L=L(d,\alpha),q=q(d,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for all <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> invariant function <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, can be approximated to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on the set <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/4}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[\psi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[\psi]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is a neural network with width <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}\left(\log(1/\epsilon)\right)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The claim follows almost immediately from Corollary <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. The only technicality that needs to be addressed is, that for all <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in K_{1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in K_{1/4}</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to ensure that all possible <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{-1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{-1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> encountered in the averaging operation in (<a href="#S4.E86" title="Equation 86 ‣ 4.2 Approximation rates for rotation invariant function spaces using frames ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">86</span></a>) will reside in some known compact set.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">To this effect, we define the centralization operator</span></p>
<table id="S4.E87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)=X-\frac{1}{n}X1_{n}1_{n}^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)=X-\frac{1}{n}X1_{n}1_{n}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(87)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which translates <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> so that the average of its columns is zero. One can easily verify that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex114" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex114.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(gX)\in K_{1/2},\quad\forall X\in K_{1/4},g\in G." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(gX)\in K_{1/2},\quad\forall X\in K_{1/4},g\in G.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Using Corollary <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ReLU approximation rates ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> we can approximate <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy by a neural network <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> with the advertised number of parameters. We then have that the ReLU network <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:=\phi\circ\mathrm{cent}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>cent</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:=\phi\circ\mathrm{cent}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies our claim because
for all <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1/4}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex115"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex115.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi](X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi](X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex115.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\mathcal{I}[f](X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi](X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\mathcal{I}[f](X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi](X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex116"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex116.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left|\int\left[f(g^{-1}X)-\psi(g^{-1}X)\right]d\mu_{X}(g)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left|\int\left[f(g^{-1}X)-\psi(g^{-1}X)\right]d\mu_{X}(g)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex117"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex117.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\int\left|f(g^{-1}X)-\psi(g^{-1}X)\right|d\mu_{X}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\int\left|f(g^{-1}X)-\psi(g^{-1}X)\right|d\mu_{X}(g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex118"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex118.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\left|f\circ\mathrm{cent}(g^{-1}X)-\phi\circ\mathrm{cent}(g^{-1}X)\right|d\mu_{X}(g)&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\left|f\circ\mathrm{cent}(g^{-1}X)-\phi\circ\mathrm{cent}(g^{-1}X)\right|d\mu_{X}(g)&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Frame averaging with a Deep Sets backbone</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While frame averaging and canonicalization has been applied directly to MLPs (see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Friedmann and
Werman (2025)</a></cite>, it seems that a more popular approach is to define an averaging approach over rotations and applying it to a permutation invariant “backbone” like Deep Sets to achieve permutation invariance. This type of strategy is discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al. ()</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In this setting we cannot currently show that any frame averaging operator will have the desired approximation rates, but we can show this is the case for some examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, let us consider point clouds with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, with a frame similar to the one defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The frame averaging operator here is of the form</p>
<table id="S4.Ex119" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex119.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[f](X)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}(X)f(R_{i}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[f](X)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}(X)f(R_{i}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}^{-1}=R_{i}^{-1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}^{-1}=R_{i}^{-1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the rotation in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math> which rotates <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the positive <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> axis. The weights <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined so that they vanish when <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> (as in this case <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}^{-1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}^{-1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is not well defined. In any case, we see that
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> resides in the subspace</p>
<table id="S4.Ex120" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex120.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{i}=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times n}|\quad X1_{n}=0_{d},\quad X_{i,2}=0\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times n}|\quad X1_{n}=0_{d},\quad X_{i,2}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which is of dimension</p>
<table id="S4.Ex121" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex121.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(V_{i})=2n-3=\dim(V_{i})-\dim(E(2))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(V_{i})=2n-3=\dim(V_{i})-\dim(E(2)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, we can obtain <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> approximation forHölder functions <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, using a model <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[\psi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[\psi]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is a Deep Sets model, using our Corollary <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For point clouds with dimension <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> a similar frame can be constructed, and similar approximation rates can be obtained, we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozdnyakov and Ceriotti (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2024)</a></cite> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A more popular <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> averaging procedure uses the singular value decomposition of the point cloud. This method was first introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al. ()</a></cite> and was also used for FAENet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duval et al. (2023)</a></cite>. A given point cloud <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is first centralized to attain translation invariance, and then the rotations are fixed by considering the SVD decomposition of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, namely</p>
<table id="S4.Ex122" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex122.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)=UDV^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)=UDV^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in O(d)</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix with non-negative entries (the singular values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a matrix whose <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> columns are orthonormal. In the case where the singular values are distinct, this decomposition is unique up to multiplication of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> by a diagonal matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> whose diagonal entries are in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus there are in total <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (in applications typically <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{d}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{d}=8</annotation></semantics></math>) different possible decompositions</p>
<table id="S4.E88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E88.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)=U_{j}DV_{j}^{T},\quad j=1,\ldots,2^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)=U_{j}DV_{j}^{T},\quad j=1,\ldots,2^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(88)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The frame suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al. ()</a></cite> is then defined by</p>
<table id="S4.E89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}[f](X)=2^{-d}\sum_{j=1}^{2^{d}}f(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></munderover><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}[f](X)=2^{-d}\sum_{j=1}^{2^{d}}f(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(89)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">It is also shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al. ()</a></cite> that the model obtained by applying this frame to Deep Sets if invariant to both rigid motions and permutations, and moreover can approximate all such functions uniformly on some compact set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This claim requires that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in</p>
<table id="S4.Ex123" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex123.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{d,n}=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\text{ the singular values of }\mathrm{cent}(X)\text{ are pairwise distinct. }\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mtext> the singular values of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> are pairwise distinct. </mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{d,n}=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\text{ the singular values of }\mathrm{cent}(X)\text{ are pairwise distinct. }\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">so that the frame in (<a href="#S4.E89" title="Equation 89 ‣ Frame averaging with a Deep Sets backbone ‣ 4.2 Approximation rates for rotation invariant function spaces using frames ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">89</span></a>) is well defined. We now prove approximation rates for this method:
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{prop}</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,n</annotation></semantics></math> be natural numbers, such that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq d+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1])</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex124" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex124.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\text{ the singular values of }\mathrm{cent}(X)\text{ are pairwise distinct, and }\|\mathrm{cent}(X)\|_{F}\leq 1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> the singular values of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> are pairwise distinct, and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}=\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}|\text{ the singular values of }\mathrm{cent}(X)\text{ are pairwise distinct, and }\|\mathrm{cent}(X)\|_{F}\leq 1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.6" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a constant <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C(d,n,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C(d,n,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for every <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder function <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Deep Sets model <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S4.Ex125" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex125.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq C\epsilon^{-\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}(1+\log(|\epsilon|)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq C\epsilon^{-\frac{nd-\dim(E(d))}{2\alpha}}(1+\log(|\epsilon|)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.7" class="ltx_p">parameters, such that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex126" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex126.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}](X)|\leq\epsilon,\quad\forall X\in\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}](X)|\leq\epsilon,\quad\forall X\in\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using (<a href="#S4.E88" title="Equation 88 ‣ Frame averaging with a Deep Sets backbone ‣ 4.2 Approximation rates for rotation invariant function spaces using frames ‣ 4 Approximation Rates for Functions Invariant to Permutations and Rigid Motions ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">88</span></a>) we see that the expressions <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex127" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex127.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)=DV_{j}^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)=DV_{j}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are the singular values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix whose <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> rows are orthonormal. Also note that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}^{T}1_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}^{T}1_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)1_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)1_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the rows of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The space of all such matrices <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with the Stiefel manifold</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex128" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex128.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}=\{V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times(n-1)}|\quad\text{ the rows of }V\text{ are orthonormal }\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext> the rows of </mtext><mi>V</mi><mtext> are orthonormal </mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}=\{V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times(n-1)}|\quad\text{ the rows of }V\text{ are orthonormal }\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Explicitly, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}^{T}=V\circ T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}^{T}=V\circ T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed linear mapping defined by setting <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}1_{n}=0_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}1_{n}=0_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by fixing some isometric mapping of the orthogonal complement of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the argument we have seen above, we see that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is in the image of the smooth function</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex129" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex129.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathbb{R}^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n},\quad h(\lambda,V)=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda)\circ V\circ T_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathbb{R}^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n},\quad h(\lambda,V)=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda)\circ V\circ T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the frobenius norm, and thus the maximal singular value, of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cent</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, is bounded by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, we know in fact that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is in the image of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> when restricted to the compact set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=[0,1]^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=[0,1]^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a compact subset of the manifold <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose dimension is given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex130" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex130.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=\dim\left(\mathbb{R}^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}\right)=d+(n-1)d-\frac{d(d+1)}{2}=nd-\frac{d(d+1)}{2}=nd-\mathrm{dim}(E(d))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=\dim\left(\mathbb{R}^{d}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{d,n-1}\right)=d+(n-1)d-\frac{d(d+1)}{2}=nd-\frac{d(d+1)}{2}=nd-\mathrm{dim}(E(d)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that for an appropriate <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex131" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex131.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K)\leq C_{1}r^{-D},\quad\forall r&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K)\leq C_{1}r^{-D},\quad\forall r&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">and since <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, it is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> for an appropriate positive <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then produce a cover of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(K)</annotation></semantics></math> by balls of radius <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, by applying <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> to a cover of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by balls of radius <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/L</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex132" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex132.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(h(K))\leq C_{1}L^{D}r^{-D},\quad\forall r&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(h(K))\leq C_{1}L^{D}r^{-D},\quad\forall r&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">We can then apply Corollary <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Approximation Rates for the Deep Sets Permutation Invariant Model ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to obtain an <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> approximation for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(K)</annotation></semantics></math> by a Deep Sets network <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{D}{2\alpha}}(1+\log|\epsilon|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\epsilon^{-\frac{D}{2\alpha}}(1+\log|\epsilon|)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters. It follows that for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\Omega_{d,n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The notation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) stands for the vector in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose entries are all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex133"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex133.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}](X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}](X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex133.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\mathcal{I}[f](X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}](X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\mathcal{I}[f](X)-\mathcal{I}[\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}](X)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex134"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex134.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2^{-d}|\sum_{j=1}^{2^{d}}f(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))-\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2^{-d}|\sum_{j=1}^{2^{d}}f(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))-\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex135"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex135.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2^{-d}\sum_{j=1}^{2^{d}}|f(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))-\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))|\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>DeepSets</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>cent</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2^{-d}\sum_{j=1}^{2^{d}}|f(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))-\psi_{\mathrm{DeepSets}}(U_{j}^{-1}\mathrm{cent}(X))|\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Bi-Lipschitz invariant models</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years there has been growing interest in designing bi-Lipschitz invariant models. Such models provide an invariant embedding of the quotient space with a bounded metric distortion, which can be useful for metric based tasks like nearest neighbor search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al. (2024a)</a></cite> or for avoiding oversquashing in graph neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sverdlov et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. Examples of recent papers discussing bi-Lipschitzness include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balan et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amir and Dym (2024)</a></cite> for bi-Lipschitz permutation invariant learning on point clouds, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dym et al. (2025)</a></cite> for the alternating group, extensions to rotations in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sverdlov et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Davidson and Dym (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sverdlov et al. (2025a)</a></cite> in the context of graph neural networks, as well as the more general max filtering approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al. (2024b)</a></cite>. We will next formally define bi-Lipschitz invariant models, and then obtain approximation rates for these models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we will consider the action on <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of a group <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> which is a closed subgroup of the group of isometries of <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then define the metric <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> on the quotient space <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> simply by quotienting by the group, as in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An invariant function <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:V\to\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:V\to\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely identified with a function <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the equation</p>
<table id="S5.Ex136" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex136.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}([x])=E(x),\quad\forall x\in V." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}([x])=E(x),\quad\forall x\in V.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following simple but useful lemma shows that for invariant functions <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, Hölder stability with respect to <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to Hölder stability with respect to the metric on the quotient space.
<span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lem}</span>
Let <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed subgroup of the isometry group of <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,d_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,d_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> be some metric space, let <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be an invariant function and let <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}:V/G\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}:V/G\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the induced function on the quotient space. Then <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder if, and only if, <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder. Moreover, <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder. Then for all <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{\prime}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{\prime}\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex137" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex137.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{Y}(f(x),f(x^{\prime}))=d_{Y}(\tilde{f}([x]),\tilde{f}([x^{\prime}]))\leq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}d^{\alpha}(x,x^{\prime})\leq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}\|x-x^{\prime}\|_{2}^{\alpha}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{Y}(f(x),f(x^{\prime}))=d_{Y}(\tilde{f}([x]),\tilde{f}([x^{\prime}]))\leq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}d^{\alpha}(x,x^{\prime})\leq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}\|x-x^{\prime}\|_{2}^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">and thus <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder and <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}\leq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}\leq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the other direction, assume <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder. Choose some <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{\prime}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{\prime}\in V</annotation></semantics></math>. there exists some <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G</annotation></semantics></math> for which</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex138" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex138.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}([x],[x^{\prime}])=\|x-gx^{\prime}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}([x],[x^{\prime}])=\|x-gx^{\prime}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">For this <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex139" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex139.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{Y}(\tilde{f}([x]),\tilde{f}([x^{\prime}]))=d_{Y}(f(x),f(gx^{\prime}))\leq|f|_{\alpha}\|x-gx^{\prime}\|_{V}^{\alpha}=|f|_{\alpha}d(x,x^{\prime})^{\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{Y}(\tilde{f}([x]),\tilde{f}([x^{\prime}]))=d_{Y}(f(x),f(gx^{\prime}))\leq|f|_{\alpha}\|x-gx^{\prime}\|_{V}^{\alpha}=|f|_{\alpha}d(x,x^{\prime})^{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">This shows that <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder and <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}\geq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}\geq|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, which concludes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>
is an invariant bi-Lipschitz mapping, with constants <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;L_{1},L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;L_{1},L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, and the mapping <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Lipschitz, respectively. Equivalently, this means that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex140" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex140.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}^{-1}d_{G}([x],[y])\leq\|E(x)-E(y)\|_{\infty}\leq L_{1}d([x],[y]),\quad\forall x,y\in V." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}^{-1}d_{G}([x],[y])\leq\|E(x)-E(y)\|_{\infty}\leq L_{1}d([x],[y]),\quad\forall x,y\in V.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A trivial approximation result for Lipschitz invariant functions <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by observing that <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=f\circ E^{-1}\circ E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=f\circ E^{-1}\circ E</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ E^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ E^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz. However, this will lead to an approximation rate whose exponent depends on the embedding dimension <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, which is generally larger than the true “intrinsic dimension” <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(K/G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(K/G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Using Corollary <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we can prove the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed subgroup of the isometry group of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:V\to\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:V\to\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invariant bi-Lipschitz mapping with constants <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> invariant subset of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact subset of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> of diameter <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex141" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex141.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K/G)\leq C_{1}r^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}},\quad\forall r&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">intrinsic</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K/G)\leq C_{1}r^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}},\quad\forall r&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exists a constant <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P(N,G,L_{1},L_{2},C_{1},d_{\text{intrinsic}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P(N,G,L_{1},L_{2},C_{1},d_{\text{intrinsic}})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for all <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and every
<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is invariant and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder with <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a ReLU networks <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> expressible with <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq P(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}\log(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq P(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}\log(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters, such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex142" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex142.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(x)-\psi\circ E(x)|&lt;\epsilon,\quad\forall x\in K" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(x)-\psi\circ E(x)|&lt;\epsilon,\quad\forall x\in K</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the maps on the quotient space <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math>, induced by the invariant maps <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E,f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E,f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math> has diameter <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}(K/G)=E(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}(K/G)=E(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is of diameter <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also have that <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder because</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex143" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex143.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}(x)-\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}(y)|\leq d_{G}^{\alpha}(\tilde{E}^{-1}(x),\tilde{E}^{-1}(y))\leq L_{2}^{\alpha}\|x-y\|^{\alpha},\forall x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}(x)-\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}(y)|\leq d_{G}^{\alpha}(\tilde{E}^{-1}(x),\tilde{E}^{-1}(y))\leq L_{2}^{\alpha}\|x-y\|^{\alpha},\forall x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">We note that this argument implicitly uses the fact that the function <math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, originally a <math id="S5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Lipschitz function defined on <math id="S5.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(V)\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(V)\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, can be extended to a <math id="S5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Lipschitz function on all of <math id="S5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, by Kirszbraun theorem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can now apply Corollary <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> to the function <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> on the compact set <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(K)</annotation></semantics></math>. Technically, to do this we need to scale <math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> by an affine transformation to ensure that <math id="S5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(K)\subseteq[0,1]^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(K)\subseteq[0,1]^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and scale <math id="S5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{f}|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This rescaling will only effect the constants in Corollary <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximation Rates for Non-Invariant Functions ‣ 3 Approximation Rates for Permutation-Equivariant Models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, and is what leads to the dependence of <math id="S5.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then obtain that there exists a neural network <math id="S5.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> of the advertised size, such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex144" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex144.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}(y)-\psi(y)|\leq\epsilon,\quad\forall y\in K/G" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}(y)-\psi(y)|\leq\epsilon,\quad\forall y\in K/G</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that for all <math id="S5.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in K</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex145"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex145.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|f(x)-\psi\circ E(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|f(x)-\psi\circ E(x)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex145.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\tilde{f}([x])-\psi\circ\tilde{E}([x])|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\tilde{f}([x])-\psi\circ\tilde{E}([x])|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex146"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex146.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}\circ\tilde{E}([x])-\psi\circ\tilde{E}([x])|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\tilde{f}\circ\tilde{E}^{-1}\circ\tilde{E}([x])-\psi\circ\tilde{E}([x])|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex147"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex147.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 5 Bi-Lipschitz invariant models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> can be applied to automatically obtain approximation rates for any bi-Lipschitz model. For the sake of concreteness, we state one specific example: bi-Lipschitz invariants using max filters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Max filters were first suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, and were later studied in several papers, examples including <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mixon and Packer (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mixon and Qaddura (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qaddura (2026)</a></cite>.
They are defined for vector space <math id="S5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, acted on by <math id="S5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a closed subgroup of <math id="S5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> (that is, a subgroup of isometries without a translation component). For a given “template” vector <math id="S5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, an invariant mapping <math id="S5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\cdot;z):V\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\cdot;z):V\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex148" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex148.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(x,z)=max_{g\in G}\langle gx,z\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(x,z)=max_{g\in G}\langle gx,z\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for a collection <math id="S5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{D})</annotation></semantics></math> an invariant max filtering mapping <math id="S5.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\cdot;Z):V\to\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\cdot;Z):V\to\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined via</p>
<table id="S5.Ex149" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex149.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E(x;Z)=\left(E(x;z_{1}),\ldots,E(x;z_{D})\right),j=1,\ldots,D." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(x;Z)=\left(E(x;z_{1}),\ldots,E(x;z_{D})\right),j=1,\ldots,D.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.3" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cahill et al. (2024b)</a></cite> it was shown that for any generic choice of <math id="S5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> templates, the mapping <math id="S5.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(x;Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(x;Z)</annotation></semantics></math> in injective. Moreover, it was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balan and Tsoukanis (2023)</a></cite> that for every <em id="S5.p16.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite</em> group <math id="S5.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, this mapping, when injective, will also be bi-Lipschitz. Thus, combining these results with Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 5 Bi-Lipschitz invariant models ‣ Quantitative Approximation Rates for Group Equivariant Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> we obtain the following corollary
<span id="S5.p16.3.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{cor}</span>
Let <math id="S5.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S5.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{intrinsic}}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> invariant subset, such that <math id="S5.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/G</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact subset of <math id="S5.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/G</annotation></semantics></math> of diameter <math id="S5.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex150" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex150.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(K/G)\leq C_{1}r^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}},\quad\forall r\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(K/G)\leq C_{1}r^{-d_{\text{intrinsic}}},\quad\forall r\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.4" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a choice of templates <math id="S5.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{2N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{2N})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S5.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S5.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for all <math id="S5.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and every
<math id="S5.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is invariant and <math id="S5.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Holder with <math id="S5.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a ReLU networks <math id="S5.p16.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> expressible with <math id="S5.p16.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq P(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}\log(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq P(1/\epsilon)^{\frac{d_{\text{intrinsic}}}{2\alpha}}\log(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters, such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex151" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex151.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(x)-\psi\circ E(x;Z)|&lt;\epsilon,\quad\forall x\in K." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(x)-\psi\circ E(x;Z)|&lt;\epsilon,\quad\forall x\in K.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.5" class="ltx_p">The constant <math id="S5.p16.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S5.p16.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p16.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, but does depend on <math id="S5.p16.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},N,Z,d_{\text{intrinsic}},\alpha,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>intrinsic</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},N,Z,d_{\text{intrinsic}},\alpha,G</annotation></semantics></math>.

Similar corollaries can be obtained for other bi-Lipschitz models, such as all bi-Lipschitz models mentioned in the beginning of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements</span>
We are grateful to Thien Le, <span id="S5.p17.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">add more!</span> for useful discussions related to this work. JWS and AS were supported by National Science Foundation (DMS-2424305). JWS was also supported by the MURI ONR grant N00014-20-1-2787. ND and SH were supported by ISF grant 272/23.</p>
</div>
